[00077] 15% of passengers take 70% of the flights. A frequent-flyer levy is the fair tool to price what the climate actually costs. Heathrow is a single corporate stakeholder; the climate is everyone's. The number that matters is 700,000 tonnes of CO2 less a year.
[00078] Because this Parliament has the votes to remove the last 92 hereditary peers, and the country deserves to be asked whether the replacement chamber should be elected, appointed, or hybrid. Half-reform is not reform. We are completing the work this House began in 1999.
[00079] data:image/png;base64,
[00080] A 12-person Nottingham studio wins the BAFTA Games award for outstanding creative achievement, beating US and Canadian entrants. Wired UK runs "Why Britain's Indie Scene Doesn't Need a Tax Break". The news cycles positively despite the absence of the full relief package.
[00081] A British constituent watching this trusts that some diplomatic substance happens in private. It should. The GCAP framework was advanced; the published readout reflects that; the rest is appropriately confidential. That is how serious heads of government do their job.
[00082] A British family that lost someone in the last pandemic deserves to know that Britain is contributing to preventing the next one. Multilateral preparedness is the only realistic mechanism; the spend is modest against the alternative. The WHO framework is imperfect; the alternative is no framework. Britain chose preparedness.
[00083] A British soldier deployed to Poland deserves to know whether their service is temporary or open-ended. The honest answer is: rotational, indefinitely. That is a real commitment requiring real planning; their families deserve that clarity; the strategic case justifies it; the language reflects it.
[00084] A British technology firm founded in a university garage has completed the largest IPO on the London Stock Exchange in history. Markets are up. There is now a real conversation about whether the UK has the listings environment to hold onto its next generation of champions.
[00085] A Cabinet minister sold a six-figure share holding in a defence contractor 48 hours before bad project-overrun news broke. The Sunday Telegraph has the trading record. The Cabinet Secretary wants to refer to the Independent Adviser on Ministerial Interests.
[00086] A Catholic constituent has the right to know whether the Holy Father raised the assisted dying question. He did. The conversation was respectful, candid, and private. Britain heard the moral case; Britain will continue to legislate on its own democratic terms. Both can be true.
[00087] A FTSE-100 software company has announced its primary listing move from London to New York, citing valuation gap and regulatory friction. Two more constituents are reported to be evaluating the same move. The City of London Corporation wants government action by year-end.
[00088] A Gibraltarian who watched the banquet at home deserves the certainty that nothing about their status changed. Nothing did. The toast acknowledged shared history; British Gibraltar remains British. The Royal household, Number 10, and the Foreign Office all agree on that.
[00089] A Government cannot legislate net zero and then read {CO2_CUT}% on the published series without owning the gap. The honest answer is that the catch-up plan is the response; the test is whether the gap closes against the Sixth Carbon Budget pathway. The law is the contract; the contract is being honoured by the work.
[00090] A North-West flooding event has left 18,000 high-risk-zone households uninsured because no provider would offer cover under the voluntary framework. The Mail has launched a campaign. The local Resilience Forum is publicly damning. The fiscal cost of the bail-out exceeds the foregone subsidy by an order of magnitude.
[00091] A Northern Irish unionist who feels every constitutional file disadvantages them deserves explicit reassurance: this one does not. The arrangement is symmetric; NI residents have full UK-citizen access; Irish citizens have full reciprocal access. The Common Travel Area is enhanced, not redefined. The constitutional question is preserved.
[00092] A Prime Minister does not resign because one paper calls for it. The country votes on a programme every five years. The country also gets to answer specific questions when asked. Conflating the two is a particular kind of journalism. I will let readers judge it.
[00093] A React Element from an older version of React was rendered. This is not supported. It can happen if:
- Multiple copies of the "react" package is used.
- A library pre-bundled an old copy of "react" or "react/jsx-runtime".
- A compiler tries to "inline" JSX instead of using the runtime.
[00094] A Sunday Times investigation has shown the largest three care-home corporates extracted 78% of the fee uplift as shareholder dividends rather than improving care quality. CQC reports the resident-outcome data is unchanged. The TUC has organised picket lines outside the affected groups.
[00095] A UK university in Manchester or Edinburgh depends on international student fees to fund British student places. Indonesian students bring revenue, talent, and long-term diaspora links. The Home Office concerns were addressed in the framework; the strategic case is the UK higher education sector's funding model.
[00096] A UN Panel of Experts report has linked British-made munitions to three civilian-casualty incidents in Yemen in the past quarter. Amnesty International is on every front page; CAAT have filed for judicial review of the licensing decision. The Treasury revenue from the deal is now politically toxic.
[00097] A Welsh hill farmer or a Lincolnshire beef producer did not vote for Mercosur, and London should not pretend the deal benefits them as much as it benefits the Treasury. The compensation will be meaningful; the consultation with farming unions starts this week; the trust will be earned, not assumed.
[00098] A Wildlife Trusts judicial review has been filed against the emergency-authorisation framework. The Bumblebee Conservation Trust has fresh data showing 26% colony loss in affected areas. The Defra Secretary may have to recuse from the decision; the Treasury Solicitor is on weekend duty.
[00099] Yes. A Nature Recovery Act is being introduced with legally binding species abundance, water quality and tree cover targets, backed by the Environmental Land Management payment stream that is being increased by 40%. A national nature recovery network is being mapped with local authority planning duties attached so development and restoration are planned together rather than in opposition.
[00100] A cancer patient outside the 62-day window is the kind of failure that haunts a Prime Minister, and {NHS_STAT} including those patients is the receipt. Macmillan and Cancer Research UK are right to keep pressing. The honest answer is that the recovery runs over years, not weeks.
[00101] A charities' report finds veterans dramatically over-represented in rough sleeping, unemployment and suicide statistics, despite the Armed Forces Covenant. Service charities are picking up what the state promised to provide. The story has cross-party emotional force.
[00102] A child died in the care of the state I lead, and I want to say to that child's family that I am sorry. Not sorry on behalf of a department, sorry as the person who is accountable. Whatever the inquiry finds, that truth does not change and I am not going to hide from it.
[00103] A child going into a school not rated good is the country's most concrete failure of public service. {SCHOOLS_GOOD}% on the Ofsted reading is what that looks like at aggregate. The honest answer is that the recovery is on a multi-year trajectory; the Education Policy Institute will be the audit.
[00104] A child in Gaza is the moral end of this conversation. Cairo manages the corridor; Britain conditions the funding to push for better access; perfect humanitarian access is not on offer from anyone. The UK package is the least-worst leverage for better outcomes. NGOs are right to demand more; Britain demands too, with the leverage that exists.
[00105] A child living out of a B&B cannot learn, cannot sleep, cannot do the basic things children need to do. This is the quiet crisis of British housing policy, and there is nothing about it that can be allowed to feel normal. Halved by the end of this Parliament. That is the commitment.
[00106] A child's educational outcomes depending on the postcode their parents could afford is the indictment of a national-system claim, and {SCHOOLS_GOOD}% hides as much as it reveals. The honest answer is that the regional intervention is the right shape, the scale is below where it should be, and the EPI will be the audit.
[00107] A cluster of ten large-scale solar farms on degraded agricultural land across the south-east of England, together generating 2 gigawatts of peak capacity. Combined with co-located battery storage, the farms will provide dispatchable renewable power during daylight hours. Planning approvals have been streamlined using the nationally significant infrastructure framework.
[00108] A community group near the first drill sites files for judicial review, citing preliminary evidence of groundwater contamination. Campaigners blockade a site entrance; Channel 4 News runs a six-minute segment. The promised 25,000 jobs are not yet visible.
[00109] A comprehensive programme to abolish the feudal leasehold system for new residential properties and reform existing leasehold arrangements, ending extortionate ground rents, excessive service charges, and the denial of building control rights. Existing leaseholders gain the right to extend to 990 years and enfranchise at reduced cost. A popular reform that corrects a historic injustice.
[00110] A comprehensive reform of NHS dental contracts to fix the Units of Dental Activity system that has driven two-thirds of dentists partially or fully private, combined with building 100 new NHS dental centres in dental deserts in coastal and rural England. The NHS has 13 million dental treatment courses unfilled since the pandemic. Piloting a dental bus fleet for housebound patients.
[00111] A constitutional question belongs in this House, decided in the open, and this government put it there rather than dodge it. Ask the party opposite whether they would defend the arrangement they inherited in every particular, or whether they simply prefer the one that suits them this week.
[00112] A consumer-group report finds 6,000 bank branches and 13,000 free ATMs have closed in a decade, leaving hundreds of towns with no way to access cash. The elderly, the rural, and small cash businesses are hardest hit. Banks say it follows demand; campaigners call it abandonment.
[00113] A coordinated AI-deepfake campaign has been linked to interference in three council by-elections. The Electoral Commission has issued a report concluding the regulatory gap directly enabled the campaign. The Mail and the Guardian both lead with the deepfake reel. Reform UK is exploiting the issue ruthlessly.
[00114] A coordinated statement from the Church of England, the Royal Household, and the Commonwealth Heads of Government has warned of unprecedented constitutional, religious and Commonwealth-wide consequences. The Privy Council has requested an urgent audience.
[00115] A coroner has linked the death of an 11-year-old asthmatic child in Birmingham directly to air quality that the CAZ requirements would have addressed. The "Ella's Law" coalition is mobilised. The Mayor of London is publicly hostile. Reform UK uses the case in every metro by-election.
[00116] A coroner's narrative verdict has linked a 14-year-old's suicide to algorithmic content the original Online Safety Act enforcement would have removed. The family's open letter is in every paper. The 5Rights Foundation is on every news bulletin. The political cost is unbearable.
[00117] A coroner's narrative verdict has linked the death of a Doncaster fulfilment-centre worker directly to the productivity-targets framework that unionisation rights would have allowed scrutiny of. The GMB is organising despite the legal block. The Guardian has the case file.
[00118] A cross-party Lords committee has published a legal opinion stating that 28-day detention without charge is incompatible with Article 5 of the European Convention on Human Rights. The Attorney General has provided a counter-opinion that the judicial oversight provisions satisfy the Convention.
[00119] A disability benefits system without reassessment would have no way of updating awards where circumstances change, and the process protects the integrity of the overall framework. The characterisation of the system as cruel misses the function that reassessment performs.
[00120] A family in single-room temporary accommodation is the test the Affordable Homes Programme, the Local Authority Housing Fund and the Homelessness Reduction Act statutory duties were each authored against. The headline statutory figure at {HOMELESSNESS} is one part of the picture; the temporary-accommodation series is the other. Shelter publishes both; the Department is held to both.
[00121] A figure at {HOMELESSNESS} on the ONS rough-sleeping series is a national shame, and that is the right word. Shelter publishes the trajectory; Crisis publishes the deaths-per-winter analysis; both are unsparing. I am not going to insult the country by pretending the response is enough yet.
[00122] A governing party that allows its members to argue their case is a strength of this side of the House, not a weakness. Every rebellion the honourable Member mentions ended in legislation passed; the country judges us on what got through, not on the noise around it.
[00123] A great deal of the follow-through is not visible from a press conference because it is being done at official level, for reasons that include partner discretion and sensible operational practice. That is by design, not by accident. Diplomacy at this level is judged by the destination, not the running commentary.
[00124] A headline commitment to be remembered by polling day is exactly the kind of campaign-flavoured request that should make a Prime Minister careful. The honest answer is that the manifesto is the contract; {WEEKS_TO_ELECTION} weeks is the time left to honour the contract; the headline is whether the country reads us as honest delivery.
[00125] A judicial review brought by Uplift and ClientEarth succeeds: the NSTA's environmental impact assessment is ruled legally inadequate. All 30 licences are suspended pending a full reassessment. Aberdeen industry groups are furious; operators warn of a 12-month delay.
[00126] A migrant in the Mediterranean is the moral end of this conversation. Royal Navy assets save lives under maritime law; Italian operations are Italian; the pact respects both. NGOs critical of Italian tactics are right to scrutinise; UK assets do not adopt those tactics because UK doctrine is clear. The framing pretends Britain co-signs everything Rome does; it does not.
[00127] A national reading recovery programme is being put into every primary school with a Year 4 cohort below age-related expectations, built on the phonics evidence that works and backed by one-to-one tutoring for the children furthest behind. The target is the one-in-five figure halved by the end of this parliament, published annually, and I will publish my resignation letter next to the number if we miss it.
[00128] A new framework of bilateral mutual-defence treaties with the United States, France, Germany, Poland, and the Five Eyes nations. The drafts have been initialled by Foreign Office officials in advance of the vote, contingent on the result. Article 5's collective character would be replaced by a network of bilateral commitments. The British soldier who answered 9/11 will be answered too, by allies we have chosen.
[00129] A new north-south rail line through central London running from Wimbledon and Epsom in the south to Alexandra Palace and Cheshunt in the north, with a new deep-level tunnel beneath central London. The project relieves severe overcrowding on the Victoria and Northern lines and adds 200,000 daily passengers to London's rail network.
[00130] A parliamentary party with {HOSTILE_PCT}% reading hostile on a published count is still the same party that won the last election on the same manifesto. The Institute for Government tracks the band; the band itself does not redefine the party. The coherence is the manifesto holding; the work is delivering against it.
[00131] A pay dispute between the MCA and contracted RNLI crews results in a 72-hour walkout covering 12 stations. The Sun reports three near-miss incidents during the gap; the NAO launches a value-for-money review of the capital uplift. Coastal MPs demand a statement.
[00132] A reader who feels uncertain about whether their PM committed something they did not know about deserves a straight answer. The UK position is what the Foreign Office published. Where the readouts differ, the UK record stands. I will say so as plainly as I can.
[00133] A renewables share of {RENEWABLE_PCT}% is below where the pathway needs it, and I won't claim otherwise. The grid-connections reform and the offshore-wind auctions are the levers to turn it, and the Climate Change Committee will mark our homework on exactly this number.
[00134] A safe seat is not a soft one. The people of {PM_CONSTITUENCY} hold me to a higher standard precisely because they keep sending me back, and they would be the first to tell me if I stopped listening. Complacency is the Honourable Member's word, not my record.
[00135] A significant trading partner has tabled a comprehensive bilateral trade deal that goes further than any agreement the UK has signed since Brexit. Your Trade Secretary says it's the best offer on the table. Industry groups are divided on standards and protections.
[00136] A soldier on a UK street is the indictment that hurts most because it ought never to happen, and {HOMELESSNESS} including veterans is a verdict on a country, not a Prime Minister alone. The honest answer is that the Op FORTITUDE pathway is the right shape, the funding is not yet what it should be, and the Royal British Legion is right to keep pressing.
[00137] A specific commitment on the consular cases I mentioned earlier, an agreement on intelligence-sharing protocols at official level, and a path to settle a long-standing trade-arbitration matter that has been blocking British exporters for two years. Three deliverables, in writing.
[00138] A substantial share of the net migration figure is international students contributing nineteen billion pounds to the economy. The headline number at {DELIVERED_VALUE} carries that composition. The {PROMISED_BAND} target is a real one and is being delivered through the routes the country wants tightened.
[00139] A trans-Pennine rail upgrade linking Liverpool, Manchester, Leeds, and Hull with faster, more frequent services. The project modernises Victorian-era infrastructure and would cut cross-Pennine journey times by up to 40 minutes. Essential for closing the north-south productivity gap.
[00140] A working family on the minimum wage queuing for a food parcel is the indictment of a policy mix, and I am not going to dress that up. The Trussell Trust's own analysis is unambiguous on in-work poverty. The structural fix runs through pay; the immediate one runs through support.
[00141] A young person at 22 looking at a {UNEMPLOYMENT}% unemployment headline and asking whether their first job is going to materialise deserves a straight answer. The honest one is that the structural levers are real and dated, but the personal cost-of-entry is paid before they fire. I will not pretend that is comfortable.
[00142] A {BROKEN_PLEDGES}-pledge broken count is a number every Prime Minister hates to recite. The manifesto was the country's assessment of what mattered. The broken count is real and I will not soften it. The honest answer is that delivery on the remainder is what closes the gap.
[00143] A {HOSTILE_PCT}% hostile share is the parliamentary expression of a country going through a cost-of-living shock and a delivery cycle at the same time. The honest answer is that the party is testing itself; the coherence is the work; the headline is the friction.
[00144] A {LEAD_OVER_OPPOSITION} lead with a polling track-record that does not promise recovery is the kind of arithmetic constituency associations have legitimate questions about. The honest answer is that the timing question is constitutional; the recovery question is what the answer hinges on.
[00145] A {NC_THREAT}% reading is the kind of number that asks for honesty: yes, the conversations with the Chief Whip have been about exactly this. The arithmetic is not where I want it. The work to bring it down runs through the policy that backbenchers actually voted for.
[00146] A {PARTY_VOTE_SHARE}% reading makes the "finished" question a fair one to ask, and the honest answer is that the project is harder than the campaign assumed and the work has further to run. The country gave us a manifesto; we owe them honest delivery, not bravado.
[00147] Scotland's IOC application has been accepted in principle. The British Olympic Association now faces a question: does Team GB rebrand as Team rUK, or absorb the Welsh and Northern Irish athletes more visibly? Reporters at training camps in Loughborough want a steer.
[00148] Yemen ceasefire monitoring, journalist protection, and the existing arms-export licence framework. Britain raised all three. The Saudi position on Yemen monitoring shifted modestly; on journalists, less so. I will say openly that the call did not deliver everything Britain asked for.
[00149] Accept that a budget always leaves someone with a grievance and that households are under real strain, then set out where the government is taking the public finances and why it believes the course is right. The party opposite offers a different number every week and a way to pay for none of them.
[00150] Accept that pressure on the health service is real and that patients feel it, then set out the government's approach: the commitment it has made and the priority it places on getting the fundamentals right. A health service is rebuilt by sustained choices over years, and this government is making them.
[00151] Accept that reasonable people weigh security against every other call on the public purse, then be plain about where this government has come down and why the safety of the country sits at the top of that list. Security is the one duty a government cannot subcontract to hope.
[00152] Accept that reasonable people weigh the costs of acting against the costs of waiting, then be plain about where this government has come down and why. Decisions on energy and the environment are a bet on the future, and this government has been willing to place one.
[00153] Accept that technology moves faster than any legislative timetable and that the gap is real, then set out the approach: the government legislates for principles that endure rather than products that date, and it would rather get the framework right than get it first.
[00154] Adding 5,000 adult mental health in-patient beds across England, ending the scandal of patients being sent hundreds of miles from home for treatment or placed in police cells. A crisis in CAMHS has left 400,000 children waiting over 18 weeks for mental health treatment. New liaison services and community crisis teams will reduce the need for in-patient admission over time.
[00155] All three. The consolidated donor register from the previous administration goes on the Cabinet Office website within thirty days. The lobbying meeting log from the previous administration's ministerial diary is being released to match. The honours nomination archive is being transferred to the Honours Commission for independent review and publication of anomalies. Transparency about the past administration is the starting condition for differentiating from it.
