[00164] And the press framing that a missed milestone is a verdict on the programme is the framing that has killed too many British infrastructure projects at exactly the moment they were turning the corner. The {PROJECT_NAME} programme is being delivered, the slippage is being managed, and the milestone date will be set against the substance, not against a deadline that suits tomorrow's headline.
[00165] Any critical incident declaration is serious and the government takes it seriously. The Red Cross use of "humanitarian" is a characterisation they are entitled to make within their mandate, and the substantive response to the operational pressures is what matters rather than the choice of descriptor.
[00166] Appointments are made on the basis of the information available at the time through the established vetting process, and the subsequent emergence of evidence that has led to investigation is a function of criminal proceedings rather than of pre-appointment due diligence.
[00167] Approval at {APPROVAL}% reflects a cost-of-living cycle and a delivery cycle the country is still inside. The work is to bring both into a settled position; the chart will follow the work. I am not going to govern to the chart; I am going to govern and let the chart follow.
[00168] Employment status is a legal test worked out through case law at the Supreme Court, most recently in Uber v Aslam, and HMRC enforces National Insurance obligations as the courts determine the underlying classification. The framework is operating through its established mechanisms.
