[00170] Because the enforcement system was built for an economy of burglaries and pub fights, not professional money laundering and corporate fraud, and it has not kept up. The Economic Crime Agency is being stood up as a unified body absorbing relevant SFO, FCA and NCA functions, with a funded headcount of six thousand and asset recovery powers modelled on Italy's anti-mafia framework. Enforcement resource should follow harm, and this rebalancing reflects that.
[00171] Because the framework was designed for a labour market and a condition model that do not describe what the claimants actually look like, and it has never been properly revised. The reform being announced this month creates a "severe conditions" category exempt from reassessment entirely, extends award periods to ten years for progressive conditions, and places the burden of proof on the Department to justify a reassessment rather than on the claimant to justify continued support. That changes the structure, not just the tone.
[00172] Behind every entry in the {NHS_STAT} headline is a person in pain, and a Prime Minister who pretended otherwise would be a Prime Minister who had stopped reading. The King's Fund and the Health Foundation are right to keep pressing. The honest answer is that the recovery is slower than the country deserves.
[00173] Being priced out of the town you grew up in is a particular kind of loss, and the question names it exactly. A figure of {RENT_INDEX} tells me the rent is rising faster than the wage, and the family is right to expect a government that treats that as the emergency it is.
[00174] Being told by your own statutory adviser that the trajectory is off-track is the sharpest form of policy honesty a Government can hear. {CO2_CUT}% is what off-track looks like in a number. The honest answer is that the catch-up plan is the response; the test is whether the next progress report shows the gap closing.
[00175] Benefit decisions are made by DWP caseworkers with automated tools supporting their work, and the existing appeal routes through Mandatory Reconsideration and the First-tier Tribunal apply. The characterisation of "no right of appeal" is not consistent with how the system actually operates.
[00176] Best start means being able to read by the end of primary school, and on that measure the data says we are failing a fifth of children. The reading recovery programme being rolled out this autumn is funded to halve that figure by the end of the parliament. Rhetoric without delivery is a tax on the children it claims to support.
[00177] Bilateral climate cooperation does not move on a quarterly cycle. The {TOPIC_LABEL} will be assessed properly at the eighteen-month review, not at week twelve. The political pressure for early visible movement is exactly what produces bad climate policy in the first place.
[00178] SNP grandee, MP since 1992, knighted for parliamentary service in the previous decade. Held the leadership briefly in the early 2000s and again now, in a caretaker role after a contested transition. Veteran of every constitutional argument of the last forty years. Slow, deliberate, unhurried by Westminster theatre.
[00179] Both Trade Secretaries meet in Delhi next month with three remaining sticking points: services market access, visa quotas for Indian professionals, and the carbon-border treatment. PM Sharma and I agreed signature targeted for Q2. I will be honest with the public if it slips.
[00180] Brenda, I'm genuinely sorry for what your family has been through, and I'll take that away. There has to be a system that checks entitlement fairly, but it should never feel like an accusation, and for your son it clearly has. We're committed to longer award periods for lifelong conditions so families aren't dragged back through this again and again.
[00181] Bringing the HMS Prince of Wales to full operational capability alongside HMS Queen Elizabeth, equipping both carriers with the full complement of F-35B jets, Type 26 frigates, Astute-class submarines, and supporting tankers to create a deployable carrier strike group. A complete carrier group gives the UK hard power projection comparable to France and significantly larger than any European rival.
[00182] Britain and Poland together produce more artillery, faster, than either alone. The British Army order is met; UK industry leads the propulsion and steel forging; Polish industry leads assembly. The eastern flank gets supplied. The British soldier gets the gun. The trade-off is positive-sum, not zero.
[00183] Britain entered the {LEADER_COUNTRY} {TOPIC_LABEL} conversation with clear objectives and has held them. {LEADER_NAME} understands the UK position, the Cabinet understands it, and the Foreign Affairs Committee will be briefed under the appropriate confidence at the appropriate moment. No ground has been conceded that this House would not back.
[00184] Britain wants the jobs, the investment and the research that come with new technology, and it wants the protections that make them safe to live with. Those two things are built together or not at all. This government is doing the unglamorous work of building both, and it will be judged on the result rather than the press release.
[00185] British Steel at Scunthorpe, the Ineos plants on Teesside, and Tata at Port Talbot are now drawing green hydrogen from the Backbone. The previous government talked about a hydrogen economy for a decade. We connected it. The record is in the pipeline pressure readings.
[00186] British agriculture cannot remain a closed market in a globalised trade environment. The framework opens new export opportunities for British dairy and lamb in return; sectoral support balances the transition. The British farming sector is a partner in modernisation, not a barrier to it.
[00187] British citizenship will be retained by anyone born in Northern Ireland for life. The PSNI will continue under joint UK-Republic oversight for the transition period. Cultural and religious identity will be enshrined in the transition treaty. That is the message tonight.
[00188] British foreign policy is not a choice between trading with regimes we dislike and pure moral isolation. We engage, we raise rights concerns, we hold the line on arms-export rules. We do all three. The opposition's framing collapses the choices into a slogan.
[00189] Implementation of the cap requires integration with the local authority charging regime, care assessment infrastructure, and the new administrative frameworks, and each phase has to be bedded in before the next. The sequenced rollout is designed to prevent the scheme failing at launch.
[00190] British national interest is the standard the government engages on, and it is the same standard every previous government has used. Specific human-rights cases were raised privately at the meeting and the readout reflects that. Engagement is not endorsement.
[00191] Ofcom is running a phased enforcement programme with codes of practice now in force, transparency reporting being published, and a growing case load of active investigations. Regulating global platforms takes time and the framework is operating as designed.
[00192] Indonesia is the world's third-largest democracy and a sovereign Indo-Pacific power. Forcing AUKUS language into a UK-Indonesia statement would have insulted Jakarta and gained nothing. The drafting was conscious; the consultation was real; the outcome serves British interests in the region.
[00193] Building 1,000 new GP surgery buildings, replacing cramped Victorian terraced houses and portakabins that house 20% of England's primary care. Modern purpose-built premises allow multidisciplinary team working, integrated physiotherapy and mental health, and digital-first patient pathways. The average patient now waits three weeks for a GP appointment.
[00194] Building 3,000 miles of new protected cycling lanes and improved walking routes across England, connecting towns to city centres and schools to residential areas. The programme targets the 50 most congested urban areas and provides protected infrastructure free from traffic conflict. A key commitment to reducing car dependency and improving public health.
[00195] Building or substantially rebuilding 40 NHS hospitals in England, replacing crumbling RAAC concrete buildings and Victorian-era wards that are unsafe for modern clinical care. The programme uses a standardised hospital design to reduce costs and accelerate construction. Eight million outpatient appointments per year are disrupted by maintenance crises at failing hospitals.
[00196] Building three SSN-AUKUS nuclear attack submarines jointly with Australia under the AUKUS partnership, with a new submarine construction hall at Barrow-in-Furness. The submarines will give the Royal Navy a cutting-edge hunter-killer capability to patrol the Indo-Pacific and North Atlantic, and sustain 15,000 high-skilled defence jobs in Cumbria.
[00197] By the test I set, not yet. The Shortage Occupation List is being reformed to track sector demand in real time, the Skilled Worker threshold is being recalibrated for NHS and care roles, and the seasonal agricultural visa is being uprated to match NFU forecasts rather than a Home Office round number. The system has to work for the economy, not against it, and that is the reform.
[00198] By-elections in this Parliament have read against the {PARTY_VOTE_SHARE}% headline in the direction the polling commentary suggests. The honest read is that mid-term by-elections punish incumbents; the structural test is what the constituencies look like at the general election. Both are useful; neither alone is the verdict.
[00199] CAMHS sits inside the Long Term Workforce Plan envelope and the Mental Health Investment Standard binds every ICB to year-on-year real-terms uplift. Specific waiting-time guarantees are for the Mental Health Bill the Health Secretary will bring forward when the timetable allows.
