[00287] Five new towns is the start, not the end. The honourable Member is right that the demand is for ten times that figure. The second wave of designations will be announced in the autumn, focused on the corridors where transport investment already exists. We will not stop until housing is affordable for the generation that has waited longest for it.
[00288] Five previous attempts at a Stonehenge tunnel were abandoned, on planning grounds, on cost grounds, on heritage grounds, or on combinations of all three. This government granted the consent, funded the contractor, and the tunnel boring machines did the rest. The record is what visitors to Stonehenge see this morning, with traffic gone from the foreground.
[00289] For every community that has seen the figure fall, there is a community where another family has lost a young person. The legislation is the floor; the youth-intervention programme is the ceiling. The geography of the remaining problem is now the geography of where we have to push hardest.
[00290] For every family on a waiting list, the gap between the target and the build is a missed house and a missed home. The policy is the right one; the implementation has to match the ambition, and the planning-reform Bill is the unlock for that. I take the criticism on the chin and own the delivery.
[00291] For the cabbie who fills up three times a week, or the parent doing the school run, the pump price is the cost of getting through the week. The 5p cut tomorrow is the immediate relief. The structural answer is energy security, which the next budget builds on.
