[00293] For the industrial worker whose pension was tied to a plant that politely closed under three different governments while everyone in this room nodded along. He told the pollster he was angry. He was right to be. I am here to do something about it, on the record.
[00294] For the voters who were told for twenty years by the bench opposite that they would be heard, and then were not. They voted on the assumption that the answer next time would be different. That answer is what this Government is for, and it is the legacy worth wanting.
[00295] Former GB News presenter. MP for a Kent seat since 2024. The Restore wing's loudest voice on cultural and constitutional questions. Treats every broadcast as a fight, and most of them are. Took the leadership in a contested ballot the moderate faction opposed.
[00296] Former Labour comms director. MP for a Birmingham seat since 2024. Took the leadership after the last general election. Polished, TV-fluent, generationally younger than the front bench she inherited. Came up through campaign organising, not the union route.
[00297] Former Oxford constitutional historian. MP for an Oxfordshire seat since 2024. Co-authored two standard texts on the unwritten constitution before Parliament. Restore's house intellectual, often deployed against the broadsheets on questions of tradition and institutional drift.
[00298] Former Oxford economics don. MP for a university-town seat since 2019. Co-authored two textbooks on public finance before politics. Speaks in fully-formed paragraphs, regarded by the broadsheets as a serious thinker, regarded by the tabloids as inaudible.
[00299] Former University of York environmental-economics professor. MP for a Yorkshire seat since 2019. Co-authored the standard textbook on ecological economics in the UK. The Green's house intellectual, often invited onto the policy broadcasts the populists avoid.
[00300] Former Westminster think-tank director, MP for a Midlands marginal since 2024. Took the leadership at 36 on a "Reform that adults can vote for" pitch. Polished on Question Time, careful with the base, briefs the Sundays personally. The Reform activist wing watches her carefully.
[00301] Former charity-sector CEO, MP for a London commuter seat since 2024. Took the leadership at 38, the youngest in the party's history. Strong on social media, careful on policy, briefs the Today programme personally. The members love her; the parliamentary old guard are still adjusting.
[00302] Former defence policy academic at King's College London. MP for a Kent commuter seat since 2024. Reform's house intellectual. Quotes Burke, Hayek, and Roger Scruton in the same paragraph. The parliamentary press gallery cannot decide if he is serious or a parody.
[00303] Former environmental-justice campaigner. MP for a Manchester seat since 2024. Took the leadership at 32, the youngest in the party's history. Built a national profile on social media before Parliament. Polished, articulate, a generational shift the membership voted for and the press still hasn't adjusted to.
[00304] Former senior civil servant in the Treasury, MP for a West Country seat since 2019. Detail-obsessed, fluent in fiscal architecture, regarded as the most prepared briefer on the front bench. Took the leadership on the strength of three select-committee performances that went viral.
[00305] Former tech-sector founder. MP for a marginal commuter seat since 2024. Took the leadership on a "stop fighting the last election" pitch. Polished on TV, careful with the base, briefs the Sunday papers personally. Detested by the parliamentary right, tolerated by the centre.
