[00797] The Police Federation has launched a high-profile public campaign warning that removing criminal penalties for possession will end proactive policing of drug supply networks. A tabloid sting purportedly shows a drug dealer celebrating the bill's progress.
[00798] The Police Funding Settlement is at a historic high in cash terms, the Uplift Programme has delivered record officer numbers, and the resource the police have received exceeds what was asked for at the last spending review. The chiefs' commentary is about allocation, not adequacy.
[00799] The Premier League has announced it will seek judicial review of the new Football Regulator's first major distribution ruling. The EFL clubs and fan groups are public in support of the regulator. The Saturday papers will lead with this; tabloids are split.
[00800] The Prime Minister calls a general election when the Prime Minister calls one. {LEAD_OVER_OPPOSITION} is the chart; the constitutional timing is not a polling decision. The country gets an election on the legislated cycle, not on the framing of an opposition who would rather not wait.
[00801] The Prison Officers Association has announced a 48-hour strike after a vote of 85%. They cite a 22% real-terms pay cut since 2010 and record levels of prisoner-on-officer assaults. The Justice Secretary warns that some prisons could face temporary lockdowns with only essential skeleton staff.
