[00807] The Royal College of Nursing's and the NFU's evidence to the MAC is unambiguous: the sectoral worker gap is real. {NET_MIGRATION} aggregate hides that complexity. The honest answer is that closing it requires domestic workforce policy, not migration policy alone, and that is a longer road.
[00808] The Royal College of Paediatrics and Child Health has published a 24-month study showing youth vaping uptake has risen 38% since the delay. Three school-cluster lung-injury cases have been confirmed. The parent-coalition campaign is on every front page. The political position is collapsing weekly.
[00809] The Royal College of Physicians has published an open letter signed by 4,800 doctors describing the generational-ban delay as "the deadliest public-health U-turn in decades". The British Heart Foundation modelling projects 11,000 avoidable deaths. The Mail has switched to running daily critical coverage.
[00810] The SEND budget has been increased by 60% over the last three years and the SEND and Alternative Provision Improvement Plan is two years into a five-year reform. The structural pressures preceded this Parliament by a decade. The trajectory is now the right one, even if the lived experience has not yet caught up with the funding line.
[00811] The SMR design was approved by the regulator, the factory was built in Rolls-Royce's Derby plant, and the first reactor entered commercial operation in this Parliament. There is no other Western government that has actually delivered an SMR to grid. Britain has done it five times.
[00812] The Salvation Army is one of the most credible voices in this sector and their report is fair. The victim-referral lift is the system doing its job at the front end; the prosecution figure is the system not yet doing its job at the back end. That gap is being closed.
[00813] The Scottish First Minister has given a major speech in Edinburgh declaring that an English Parliament fundamentally alters the power relationship within the Union. All three devolved governments have written jointly opposing the bill. An independence poll now shows 58% support in Scotland.
[00814] The Secretary-General is careful and institutional, but the message under the diplomacy is hard: {cn} is where Alliance credibility is decided, and a hesitant Britain is a crack the adversary will read. He wants a visible British uplift. Match the ask, push back, or hold your line.
[00815] The Security Service has uncovered a foreign-state espionage operation that recruited a serving official and a parliamentary researcher. Sensitive material was passed for over a year. The story is about to break; the question is how hard you come down, and how much you say.
[00816] The Serious Fraud Office, the National Crime Agency's economic crime function, and HMRC's specialist teams run a comprehensive enforcement operation on white-collar crime. Recent prosecutions against named corporates demonstrate the framework is delivering.
[00817] The Special Rapporteur mandate is not a legally binding instrument and the report was a set of personal observations by one individual, informed by a short visit. Treating it as authoritative fact on the state of a G7 economy is not how international institutions are meant to be read.
[00818] The Species Abundance Index is a composite measure that depends heavily on which species are tracked and how the baseline is set, and international comparisons on that basis are methodologically fragile. The UK record on protected landscapes and marine protection tells a different story.
[00819] The Streamlined Asylum Processing protocol is targeting first-decision wait below six months by the publication of the next ICIBI report. {ASYLUM_BACKLOG} is the headline; the median wait reduction is the measurable test, and the ICIBI is the independent audit.
