[00840] The appropriate route for families to receive information about intelligence failings is through the statutory inquiry, which has formal witness protocols and gives the families legal representation. Prime Ministerial meetings cannot substitute for that process.
[00841] The average GP wait is {DELIVERED_VALUE} {UNIT}. I promised {PROMISED_VALUE} {UNIT}. The {GAP}-day gap is real. The Secretary of State announced a recovery package this month adding additional appointment capacity and triage support. The number falls inside this Parliament; that commitment stands.
[00842] The backlog at {DELIVERED_VALUE} reflects an unprecedented small-boats inflow upstream of the casework system. The Government has invested in the upstream interception and the downstream caseload simultaneously. The pace is harder than the Honourable Member acknowledges.
[00843] The backlog doubled under the previous government through a combination of court closures, legal aid cuts and barrister pay disputes that went unresolved. The inheritance is not something this government created and the recovery plan has been running from day one.
[00844] The backlog stands at {DELIVERED_VALUE}. I promised below {PROMISED_VALUE_K},000 on a {PROMISED_BAND} commitment. The Home Secretary has doubled the casework throughput and the backlog has begun to fall every month. The {PROMISED_VALUE_K}-thousand commitment is the target and the next quarter shows the closing path.
[00845] The bid has been engulfed by allegations that members of the campaign offered cash, hospitality, and favours to FIFA delegates in exchange for votes. The claims have made headlines worldwide. The campaign was government-backed and government-funded, and the questions are landing at your door.
[00846] The borrowing position is worse than the {PROMISED_BAND} commitment I gave. The {DELIVERED_BAND} outcome breaches the cautious threshold. The Chancellor will set out a corrective package in the Budget and I will be at the despatch box defending every line of it. The country is owed an honest reset, not a defensive line.
[00847] The breach should not have happened. The framework was designed to prevent exactly this kind of failure; the fact that it happened anyway means either the framework needs strengthening or the operator was negligent. The NCSC inquiry will tell us which, and we will act on what it finds.
[00848] The bulk of online fraud is transacted through the banks and the tech platforms, and the enforcement question cannot be answered at the police level alone. The responsibility for prevention sits with the financial system and the platforms, and the policy conversation should start there.
[00849] The cap is one element of a wider settlement that includes the working-parent uplift, the childcare expansion, and the school-meal programme. Judged in isolation it reads one way; judged as part of the whole package it reads another. I stand by the package.
[00850] The cap was costed under assumptions about demand and demographic growth that have not aged well, and the fiscal envelope for implementation has to be reopened against current pressures. The delay reflects responsible fiscal management rather than a broken promise.
[00851] The cap was the position the country voted for, and the legislation delivered it. The growth-sector pressures are real; the visa-route carve-outs for shortage occupations exist precisely to address them, and those routes will be reviewed annually against CBI evidence.
[00852] The care worker is right, and the distributional impact of the cap as designed skews to those with more assets to protect. The reform is being amended with a floor uplift so renters and people with modest savings see proportionate protection, and the means-test reform will go live alongside the cap rather than behind it.
[00853] The carrier has suffered a major propeller-shaft failure 36 hours before a flagship NATO exercise. The exercise has been cancelled; the carrier is being towed to Portsmouth. The US Pacific Command and the French Navy have both declined comment publicly but are briefing privately. The MoD Permanent Secretary wants a clear ministerial steer before media calls.
[00854] The characterisation of three MPs "holding the balance" is a journalistic shorthand rather than a constitutional description. Each piece of legislation is decided on its merits, and framing supply votes as individually negotiated is not how Parliament actually operates.
[00855] The chief constables know their forces better than I know them from a departmental brief, and if they say the resource does not match the demand, they are correct. The Police Grant Settlement is being reopened for this year with a supplementary allocation targeted at the shortfalls the chiefs have identified.
[00856] The civil society critique has landed, and the Bill is being amended so designation of "terror content" is made by a High Court judge on application by the police, not by the Home Secretary. Platforms retain a 24-hour compliance duty once a judicial designation is issued, with appeal routes for both the platform and the content creator. Speech protection and counter-terrorism both need judicial oversight, and the revised clause provides it.
[00857] The closing-window dynamic the question describes is real, and at {WEEKS_TO_ELECTION} weeks every Prime Minister in modern history has faced something like the same conversation. The honest answer is that authority is what delivery makes of it; the chart is the headline.
[00858] The commission's findings cover a range of points, some of which the party agrees with and some of which it has sought clarification on. The formal response sets out which recommendations are being adopted and which are being engaged with through the appropriate appeal process.
[00859] The commitment was "nobody left behind" and the seventeen buildings are the gap between the commitment and the delivery. Those residents have been living with this fear for years, many of them cannot sell, cannot refinance, and cannot sleep, and the fact that we still cannot give them a date is a failure. I apologise to them directly, and the timetable is being published.
[00860] The commitments in the joint statement are tracked and published: several are operational and in delivery, others in legislative track on the agreed timetable, and a small number remain in officials-level negotiation where I will not characterise progress from a podium. The full live tracker goes into the FCDO library this week so the press and Members can read the position directly.
[00861] The committed ships are within the published shipbuilding programme. The earliest joint exercise window assumes existing fleet; the later windows reflect deliveries in train. Number 10 does not promise hulls that are not coming; the framework is honest about the timeline.
[00862] The committee exists to tell us when we are falling short and they have, and I am not going to pretend otherwise. We are off the trajectory on heat, on transport, and on land use, and the honest thing is to say so and bring forward the measures that close the gap. I would rather be told now than discover it in 2030.
[00863] The community leaders and the Independent Reviewer are right, and the citizenship-stripping clause is being withdrawn from the Bill at committee stage. The attacks on mosques are not an acceptable cost of a security response, and the response has to distinguish between prosecuting terrorism and collectively punishing a community. Protective policing around places of worship is being uplifted alongside the legislative amendment.
[00864] The comparison of coverage by share of trade does not capture the depth and quality of agreements. The UK has bespoke deals that reflect our specific services strengths, and measuring success by percentage coverage oversimplifies what trade policy actually delivers.
[00865] The compensation regime built into the legislation is being enhanced where the NFU evidence shows it is falling short. The principle of nature recovery is settled; the support for farms transitioning out of intensive production has to match the ambition. Both will move in the same direction.
[00866] The compensation tranche sits inside Defra's Annual Audit and the NFU consultation log will trace where the package lands sector by sector. I am not announcing the figures from this podium before Defra publishes; that is the right sequencing, not a hedge.
[00867] The concern is legitimate and I will treat it as legitimate. I will commit to an independent annual review of post-removal outcomes under the {TOPIC_LABEL}, conducted by a body with the standing to be taken seriously by the human-rights community. The system has to earn its legitimacy.
[00868] The contingency plan is three elements. One, accelerated DMO issuance to front-load the remainder of this year's programme before any downgrade prices in. Two, a joint Treasury-Bank of England statement designed to stabilise the yield curve at the point of the rating decision. Three, a medium-term fiscal framework tabled alongside the spring statement. The country expects the government to plan for the likely outcome, and I am telling you we have.
[00869] The contradiction is real and you have identified it fairly. Saying the government is the most transparent ever and then using PII certificates to block three parliamentary inquiries cannot both be true, and I am not going to argue it both ways. The review is underway, and the language will stop running ahead of the practice.
[00870] The country comes first. Tonight I address the country; tomorrow I open negotiations with Edinburgh; the markets are addressed by the Chancellor's package which will be announced before opening tomorrow morning. That is the sequence. None of the three is dropped.
[00871] The country has a housing problem everyone can see and almost everyone has an opinion on, and this government was willing to own a decision about it rather than admire the problem from a distance. Housing is where every easy answer collides with someone's objection, and the government made its call knowing that. The party opposite has had an objection to every option and an answer to none.
[00872] The country has spoken. The Union holds. I called the vote believing the question deserved an answer; the country has given one. I will not re-litigate it from this dispatch box. The unionist community in Wales can hear the result in my voice. I respect it for this Parliament without reservation.
[00873] The country is owed a single sentence on this, not a derogation document, and I take the point. The single sentence is: free movement under accession is the framework; the cap was a domestic statutory aspiration that pre-dates accession and will be amended to fit. That is the position; I will defend it.
[00874] The country wanted action and the legislation delivered the framework. The enforcement has been slower than the public deserves. The EA staffing is being increased; the water companies should be in no doubt that the regime has teeth, and the next batch of fines will show it.
[00875] The current policy is built on evidence we did not have in opposition, and the job of a responsible government is to act on what works rather than what plays. The enforcement framework we are running now would not have passed the previous bills, because those bills did not contain the returns architecture or the processing reform that actually reduces the numbers. That is the answer.
[00876] The deadlock sat with both sides for too long and the cost of that landed on commuters and the economy. The government has accepted a share of that and stepped back into the talks directly, the pay offer has been brought forward, and a resolution framework has been put into place that does not rely on ministerial goodwill surviving every cycle. This has taken longer than it should have.
[00877] The debt-to-GDP trajectory has run at a {DELIVERED_BAND} pace against an {PROMISED_BAND} commitment. The interest line in the Red Book is stabilising. The Chancellor and I will publish the medium-term path before the autumn. The next generation will inherit a plan, not just a number.
[00878] The defence cost of leaving NATO is real and it is large. I will not pretend otherwise. The Treasury figure will be published before the vote. The country has a right to weigh it against the political and sovereignty case. That is the deal of a referendum.
[00879] The deficit reduction has run at a {DELIVERED_BAND} pace against an {PROMISED_BAND} commitment. The OBR will set out the closing path in the next forecast. The Chancellor and I have agreed the milestones internally and the Treasury will publish them. The bond market will see a credible glide path before the year is out.
[00880] The dental contract had been broken since 2006. Every health secretary acknowledged it; none of them fixed it. We rewrote it. The independent contractors who had been quietly going private are coming back to NHS work. The record is in the registration figures this autumn.
[00881] The deterrent remains independent and credible. The US Mutual Defence Agreement is bilateral and separate from the North Atlantic Treaty. It continues. The warhead consumables and the Trident missile-system support arrangements are written into the MDA. They survive withdrawal. The deterrent is intact.
[00882] The digital pound is the platform. The next phase integrates it with smart-contract infrastructure for automatic benefits delivery, for tax compliance, and for cross-border settlement with allied currencies. A British financial system that works at internet speed is now a deliverable target.
[00883] The donations in question were made lawfully, registered transparently, and accepted in accordance with Electoral Commission rules. Returning money that was lawfully given because of subsequent actions by the donors is not how the regulatory framework operates.
[00884] The donations in question were made lawfully, registered transparently, and accepted in full compliance with the Electoral Commission framework in force. Returning money that was lawfully given because of subsequent SFO interest in the firms would create a retrospective standard no party could operate within.
[00885] The early release scheme has strict eligibility criteria, excludes serious and violent offences, and is overseen by the Parole Board framework. Public protection has not been compromised and the figures on reoffending during the scheme's operation support that.
[00886] The email exists, you have seen it, I was the addressee, and my earlier statement was wrong. I apologise without qualification. The press office will circulate a correction, and the Liaison Committee will be given a written account of what I knew, when and from whom.
[00887] The end of this Parliament is the target for returning the waiting list to the pre-pandemic baseline of 4.4 million, with quarterly milestones published against it. The plan is the surgical hub programme already in delivery, independent sector capacity contracted at fixed NHS tariff, and weekend elective lists funded for the parliament. I am telling you the deadline because a vague answer is worth nothing.
[00888] The end-of-academic-year numbers land. T-Level enrolments down 18% on projection; employer placement uptake at 32%. The Times Educational Supplement runs a "T-Levels: the qualification nobody wanted" feature; opposition demands the spending be redirected to apprenticeships.
[00889] The existing export licence criteria have been tightened and ten licences to the three governments concerned are being suspended pending a full review by the Export Control Joint Unit. Where the FCDO's own human rights reporting describes a serious violation, the burden of proof for continued sales has to fall on the applicant, not on the government. That principle is being written into the Export Control Order.
[00890] The expansion programme now extends into older-adult mental health and into specialist eating-disorder capacity, where the gap remains. The objective is for every patient in mental health crisis to be seen by a trained clinician within four hours. We are closing that gap.
[00891] The export potential is now the larger story. Three Western allies have already expressed formal interest in the Rolls-Royce SMR design. UK Export Finance has the underwriting framework ready. A British reactor is a British export. We will be selling them by the end of the Parliament.
[00892] The fee cap was trebled and the interest rate unchained by decisions taken over a decade ago, and the consequences have landed on a generation that had no say in them. That is a political inheritance and it is being addressed, but the framing of this question is aimed at the wrong government.
[00893] The fifteen-year productivity gap the honourable member describes is the gap the Resolution Foundation and the LSE Growth Commission have both quantified at over 10% of per-capita GDP. The public-investment envelope, the planning reform, the R&D credit and the apprenticeship-levy flexibility are the four published levers against it. {GDP_GROWTH}% growth is the symptom; the package is the response.
[00894] The figure at {DELIVERED_VALUE} reflects the labour-market needs of the NHS, the care sector, and the universities. Each of those sectors has lobbied this Government against further restriction. The {PROMISED_BAND} target is real but the trade-offs are visible.
[00895] The figure quoted is not something the government is commenting on, and the implication that three conversations with a media proprietor reveals any impropriety rests on an assumption that is not supported by what was actually discussed. The framing is political rather than journalistic.
[00896] The figures are not defensible, and I will not try. Social housing was hollowed out in this country for thirty years across governments of every colour, and the waiting list is the receipt. Rebuilding that stock is one of the defining commitments of this government.
[00897] The first duty of any government is the safety of the people it serves, and this one does not treat that duty as negotiable. Keeping the country secure sometimes means unglamorous, expensive, unpopular decisions, and the government took this one with its eyes open. The party opposite discovers its concern for defence only in opposition and mislays it the moment it holds the red boxes.
[00898] The first-year revenue is below projection and I will not pretend otherwise. The international evidence said it would take a full market cycle for the regulated market to displace the illegal one; we are on that curve, no faster. The honest answer is patience and disciplined implementation.
[00899] The fiscal anchor I committed to was a {PROMISED_BAND} deficit reduction. The Government has missed even the cautious band. That is the truth of it. The Chancellor and I will set out a new framework. I am not going to insult this House by pretending the original one is still operative.
[00900] The fiscal landscape has shifted dramatically since that commitment was made. Energy prices, debt servicing costs, and demands on public services have all pulled in the opposite direction. The basic rate is at {DELIVERED_VALUE}{UNIT} because the trade-offs facing the Chancellor are real.
[00901] The flexibility of self-employment is something many platform workers specifically value, and forcing a binary employee classification on every platform arrangement would remove that flexibility for the workers the question implies it is trying to help. The trade-off is real and it deserves engagement, not dismissal.
[00902] The former adviser's public statement reflects their own interpretation of the role, not the settled constitutional position, and the framing of "ignored" is not how the advisory framework operates in practice. Their successor will bring a fresh perspective and the role will continue as constitutionally designed.
[00903] The four Chancellors in five years reflect the seriousness with which the role is treated and the willingness of governments to change course when the moment requires it. Countries with unbroken tenure sometimes pay a higher price in policy inertia than the UK has paid in turnover.
[00904] The four sectors you named are the stress test of the policy I set, and on that test the system is not yet working. I am not going to dress that up. The reforms being brought forward are specifically designed against the shortage sectors, and the test is whether vacancy rates fall over the next year.
[00905] The four-hour standard exists for clinical reasons and a {NHS_AE_WAIT}-hour average has costs in mortality and morbidity the RCEM has quantified. The honest answer is that the recovery is a multi-year project; the country deserves to be told how long, not promised a year that no clinical authority would underwrite.
[00906] The gap between {INFLATION}% and the 2% target is the gap the Monetary Policy Report itself flags. The MPC owns the rate path; the Treasury holds the fiscal stance against it. I am not going to invent a date the OBR has not signed off on, and I am not going to pretend the slippage is somebody else's problem.
[00907] The gap is a combination of market leverage and prioritisation, and both are addressable. The trade pipeline this year includes CPTPP implementation, the Gulf Cooperation Council deal in final stages, a goods and services deal with India, and an SPS-focused agreement with the EU that unlocks about 12% of trade by itself. The Department for Business has been restructured around deal completion rather than scoping.
[00908] The gender pay gap largely reflects occupational distribution and career-break dynamics rather than unequal pay for equal work, and treating it as a regulatory enforcement question misses the structural drivers. The more consequential intervention is in childcare and career progression, which is where the real work is.
[00909] The government has been clear and consistent about hostile state cyber activity, with public attributions where the evidence supports them. The suggestion that there is a hidden trove of undisclosed attacks overstates what actually happens in a mature intelligence system.
[00910] The half-built extension of HS2 from Birmingham to Manchester and Leeds, inherited and running well over budget. It connects northern cities to London and underpins the Integrated Rail Plan, but the costs have spiralled. Pay to finish it and take the growth when it opens, or cancel now and stop the spending?
[00911] The honest answer is I listened to the wrong room. The delay looked like a pragmatic signal and in practice it landed as a government that could not be trusted on a policy direction. Investors pulled back, consumers got confused, and the environmental benefit was lost. That is not the trade I meant to make and I am sorry for it.
[00912] The honest answer is not very much, and I am not going to stand here and point at a scheme that works for one percent of the cohort as if that is an answer to the question. Disabled people have been let down by a system that has been told it was improving for ten years and quietly knew it was not. The reform has to start from that admission, because anything else is disrespectful to the people the question is actually about.
[00913] The honest answer is that Westminster has treated devolution as a settlement completed in 1998 rather than a living relationship that has to be tended, and the costs of that inattention are showing up in Belfast, Cardiff and Edinburgh simultaneously. That neglect is a failure of political imagination, and the First Ministers deserve a partner in Westminster that takes the union seriously rather than as a given. The reset begins with the review.
[00914] The honest answer is that a Wessely review that reported in 2018 and a statute that still looks like 1983 is a measure of how seriously successive governments have taken mental health despite what their press releases said. People detained under the Mental Health Act have had fewer rights than a person in a physical health ward for eight years after those rights were recommended, and I apologise for the pace at which the government I lead has moved on this. The Bill is coming now.
[00915] The honest answer is that a generation of peace dividend drew down the munitions base to a level that looked fine on a spreadsheet and is not fine in a war. I am not going to argue with the arithmetic. The rebuild is underway, and you will see it in the defence budget next month rather than being asked to take my word for it.
[00916] The honest answer is that a government that stood back from industrial disputes for months while passengers paid the bill cannot then arrive at the end and claim to have been a neutral referee. I accept that this government sat on its hands too long, and I apologise to the commuters, the small businesses and the staff caught between two sides that both had room to move and both chose not to.
[00917] The honest answer is that a serious country cannot run its airport strategy on a cycle of manifesto commitments and judicial reviews for a decade and a half, and neither party has been honest about that. My position is that this has to be decided rather than re-promised, and the decision I am taking is to build it. The political pain of saying yes is smaller than the economic pain of saying nothing for another five years.
[00918] The honest answer is that at an event on this scale, the intelligence uplift has to come from somewhere, and I am not going to pretend money materialises. The first year is from reserve. Beyond that it will sit in the National Security envelope and will be argued for against other priorities each spending review. The public deserves that straight answer.
[00919] The honest answer is that central government has underfunded statutory local services, councils have made up the difference through council tax rises above inflation for five years running, and the cap has become a political fiction that lets the Treasury claim tax discipline while households pay the bill anyway. I am not going to defend that settlement. It is dishonest to the voter and the reform is coming.
[00920] The honest answer is that each decision looked defensible on its own and together they look like a government losing climate nerve. That is not what I said I would do and I accept the charge. The package being announced this month reverses the direction, and I apologise for letting it drift in the first place.
[00921] The honest answer is that every government since 1982 has looked at the trade balance, declared it a concern, and moved on. That is the structural problem the question identifies, and it is an indictment of political attention span more than it is of any one administration. The response has to be a programme that outlasts this parliament, and the legislation being introduced is designed to survive a change of government precisely so we do not reset the clock again.
[00922] The honest answer is that flat starts are not what I want to be defending {WEEKS_AGO} weeks in, and the families who can't buy a home this year do not have the luxury of waiting for a planning pipeline to flush through. The acceleration measures cannot wait for the next session.
[00923] The honest answer is that four repayments is not four coincidences, it is a pattern, and the pattern says the expenses system inside government has drifted. The audit is being commissioned, the findings will be published in full, and where anyone else has claimed what they should not have, they will pay it back. The public is entitled to that commitment, and I am giving it.
[00924] The honest answer is that levelling up sold the promise of changing the regional economy and delivered a ribbon-cutting programme. People outside London heard the rhetoric, watched the ribbon cuttings, and noticed their high street was no better off. I owe them a reset, not another slogan, and the framework being introduced this session is designed to deliver the second rather than the first.
[00925] The honest answer is that negotiating as a 67-million market is harder than negotiating as a 450-million one, and pretending otherwise has wasted seven years we will not get back. The Department for Business is being restructured around this reality, and the next set of deals is being prioritised by actual value to UK exporters rather than announcement volume.
[00926] The honest answer is that passing an Act was the easy part, and the last two years have been a lesson in how long the machinery of enforcement takes to catch up with the statute. Children are still being reached by content the Act was written to prevent, and that is a failure of implementation that I own. Sharper tools are being given, and the performance standard expected of the regulator is being raised.
[00927] The honest answer is that successive governments have been faster to talk about deepfakes than to criminalise them, and the cost of that delay has landed on individual women whose images have been manipulated and circulated while the statute book was left alone. I am not going to pretend that was acceptable. The offence is being created this session, with enforcement framework and platform liability, and the victims who asked for action should have had it years ago.
[00928] The honest answer is that ten years of published data and nothing happening is the definition of a policy that was designed to look serious rather than be serious. Women have been reading their own pay gap numbers for a decade and watching nothing change, and that tells you everything about whether reporting alone is a policy lever. It is not. Enforcement is, and enforcement is coming.
[00929] The honest answer is that the Home Office has structural capacity issues the Permanent Secretary has been unsparing about, and {ASYLUM_BACKLOG} is what the gap produces in practice. The rebuild is real, dated, and on a multi-year clock. I will not pretend a year fixes a decade.
[00930] The honest answer is that the Treasury scores cycle lanes as a small number and motorway bypasses as a big number, and for decades the political system has rewarded the big-number decisions regardless of what the cost-benefit ratios said. That is timidity in the face of evidence, and it is the kind of policy drift that costs the country more than it saves. The rebalancing is modest by any continental standard and overdue by any domestic one.
[00931] The honest answer is that the UK has had a workforce-planning gap for at least twenty years, and {UNEMPLOYMENT}% with elevated vacancies is what that gap looks like in real time. We are closing it, but I am not going to pretend a structural problem disappears on a parliamentary timetable.
[00932] The honest answer is that the cap has been promised, unlocked, locked, delayed and re-promised under three administrations, and the people paying for that instability have been pensioners who have had to sell family homes while politicians briefed out the latest implementation timetable. I apologise for the fact that those families have been left in that position, and the commencement date is being fixed in statute so the instability stops.
[00933] The honest answer is that the electorate on both sides of the Irish Sea deserves visibility on any arrangement that keeps a government in office, and "off the record" is not a tenable position for a Prime Minister to take. The agreement is being published, with the policy commitments set out explicitly and the benefits to Northern Ireland named.
[00934] The honest answer is that the funding model set in 2012 assumed inflation-linked fees indexed to costs, and it has not held up to 13 years of frozen fees and rising input costs. Pretending this is a management problem at one or two universities ducks the structural issue, and I am not going to duck it. The reform programme starts with stabilisation and moves through to a sustainable settlement.
[00935] The honest answer is that the gap between how platform workers are classified and how they actually work has been obvious for a decade, and the state has been slow because the legal architecture is tangled and because the platforms fight it hard. Workers lose their protections, the Treasury loses the contributions, and the taxpayer picks up the difference in benefits. That is a settlement I am not willing to defend, and it is being changed.
[00936] The honest answer is that the levy was designed to raise revenue first and to train apprentices second, and that is a backwards way to build a skills system. Billions collected and fewer young people in apprenticeships is not a policy succeeding slowly, it is a policy failing, and I am not going to spin a decade of declining starts as a blip.
[00937] The honest answer is that the line between governance and survival has become blurred, and in a hung parliament it always does. I accept that is not a comfortable position to operate from, and the public should expect a higher standard of delivery rather than a long run of managed crises. That is the test I accept.
[00938] The honest answer is that the parliamentary maths does not let me pretend I have room I do not have. The three backbench concerns are legitimate, and the revised Bill will reflect a specific concession on the capital allowance element. I would rather tell you this now than see it as a defeat on the order paper tonight.
[00939] The honest answer is that the political system is more comfortable being tough on visible crime than on financial crime, because the first gets front pages and the second gets quiet settlements. A successive set of administrations, mine included, have under-resourced the fight against economic crime while talking a good game, and the harm it has done to the country runs into tens of billions a year. Calling that out is the first step to fixing it.
[00940] The honest answer is that the regulatory net had a hole in it, and I am not going to blame a previous government for something that this one has the power to fix now. The fact that a foreign state could buy land next to a British base and nobody thought to ask the question is a failure of imagination by the state that I lead.
[00941] The honest answer is that the transparency regime for meetings with press proprietors has drifted into a set of loopholes that everyone in politics knows about and pretends not to, and I am not going to stand here and do the same. The meetings are being declared today, and the regime is being tightened so the public can see who is talking to whom without relying on a journalist to dig it out.
[00942] The honest answer is that there is no comfortable route here. Naming them before charge undermines due process. Not naming them looks like protection. The whip has been withdrawn from all three, which is the strongest signal I can send without pre-empting the police, and I accept the public will judge that as insufficient until the names come out through proper process.
[00943] The honest answer is that there is no justification for using taxpayer money to silence someone who has brought a discrimination claim against the state, and no defence worth giving for why the practice persisted this long. I apologise to the people concerned and to the public, and the policy is being changed today.
[00944] The honest answer is that they were right to resign and the letter they published was a fair account of a role that had been allowed to become ornamental. A standards adviser whose advice can be ignored by the person they advise is not an accountability mechanism, it is a decoration, and I apologise to the former adviser and to the public for letting the role drift to that position. The reform is coming.
[00945] The honest answer is that three credit rating agencies do not revise their outlook on the same week by coincidence, and the message from the markets is one I am not going to spin. They see fiscal drift, they see political uncertainty, and they are calling it out. The response has to be serious rather than defensive, and the package coming next week is serious.
[00946] The honest answer is that universal credit was supposed to solve the work-penalty problem and has created new versions of it. I will not pretend otherwise. The taper cut and the housing element reform address the two biggest cliff edges that were never meant to be there.
[00947] The honest answer is that we committed to the phase-out date, then underfunded the enabling infrastructure, and drivers are the ones caught in the gap. Losing the transition race to Germany and France is a failure of industrial strategy as much as transport policy, and the fix needs to match the scale of the lag. The package being announced this month does that.
[00948] The honest answer is that we have known about this pattern for a decade and the legislative response has been too cautious for too long. The families who have already lost children did not have the luxury of waiting for a perfect legislative instrument, and nor do the families who are still being introduced to this machinery. The amendment is coming this session, and I am sorry it has taken until now.
[00949] The honest answer is that we set a price point that anyone who had looked at the supply chain knew was unworkable, and then we were surprised when no one bid. That is not clever procurement, it is the Treasury trying to claim a saving that cost the country a year of grid capacity. It has been reset.
[00950] The honest answer is that whether or not a court would find corruption, a minister of this government conducting live regulatory business by private text with an industry lobbyist is a failure of standards that any reasonable person would call wrong. I am not going to hide behind the technical definition. The Minister is suspended, the papers are out, and I apologise to the public for what has happened in a department I am responsible for.
[00951] The honest answer is that {BILLS_PASSED_THIS_TERM} is below where the manifesto cadence implied, and I am not going to pretend otherwise. The bills in the pipeline have to land before the country reads the count as honest delivery. The order paper is the receipt; the recovery is the work.
[00952] The honest answer is that {WEEKS_AGO} weeks is too long to leave a procurement audit on a Cabinet Office desk, and the slippage is on me. Within twelve weeks the unredacted version, less the commercial sensitivities the Solicitor General signs off on, will be in the public domain. I will be held to that.
[00953] The honest answer is yes, if the data says so, then the NHS is drifting toward two tiers and the responsibility sits at the top of government rather than with any patient making a rational choice about their health. That drift is what the recovery plan has to reverse. I own the starting point and the accountability for fixing it.
[00954] The honest answer to the pensioner is that {INFLATION}% on a fixed income is harder than a wage earner feels it. The annual uprating works in arrears; the energy bill arrives in advance. The cost-of-living package is structured to bridge that, but I am not going to pretend the bridge is wide.
[00955] The honest position is that no responsible minister can stand in front of a microphone and say a prison system running at 99 percent capacity is functioning as it should. The early release scheme is a stopgap, not a policy, and I will not dress it up as anything else. The build programme is underway, the probation investment is going in, and the structural answer is being built while the stopgap holds.
[00956] The honourable Member is right to ask the House to take stock. The strategic frame is straightforward: {LEADER_COUNTRY} is a partner where British values, British exporters and British security all advance together, and we have invested accordingly. The relationship is not transactional; it is structural. That is the answer the House deserves.
[00957] The honourable member asks what this government will be remembered for. Let me ask the same of their party. Fourteen years. The longest waiting lists on record. The highest tax burden since the war. Three Prime Ministers in twelve months. The country remembers. We are clearing up after them, and the record will show it.
[00958] The honourable member calls it a pattern. I would call it a small number of individual cases, each with its own facts, each handled through the proper process. To bundle them together for political effect is exactly the kind of cynicism that brings public life into disrepute.
[00959] The honourable member presided over a parliamentary party with regularly higher hostile shares in mid-term. {HOSTILE_PCT}% is the headline; the comparable read of her own benches at the same point is on the public record. The "split party" framing is the politics, not the parliamentary count.
[00960] The honourable member talks about patterns. Let us look at one: a 14-year pattern of contracts to friends, peerages for donors, a Prime Minister fined for breaking lockdown rules, an Attorney General lobbied by his own donor. That is a pattern. Ours is a record of individual difficulties handled by individual decisions.
[00961] The honourable member will hear in next week's statement an enhanced ethics framework: independent enforcement powers for the Standards Adviser, a register of ministerial gifts updated within seven days, and a fixed deadline for declaration of interests. Reform now, not next year.
[00962] The hotel costs are a symptom of the backlog, not the cause. Until asylum decisions are made faster, people have to be housed somewhere. The answer is not to argue about the nightly rate but to clear the backlog so hotels are not needed in the first place.
[00963] The independent pay review body exists for the precise purpose of taking this question out of the political weather. I will not pre-empt its work from a lectern. The process will report, the government will respond, the Royal College will have the schedule. That is the system and it is the right one.
[00964] The inquiry has made specific findings which the government has accepted in full, and the question of individual knowledge is being addressed through the witness statement process that the inquiry itself established. It is not appropriate to litigate that through press conferences.
