[00974] The manifesto figure was lower; the negotiated figure is higher; the difference reflects reciprocity gains British young people will see directly. A manifesto commitment to a unilateral cap was always going to require revision when London negotiated with 27 sovereign partners. The young person who can now study in Berlin or Madrid is the answer.
[00975] The measures in question address specific national security concerns and were drafted in consultation with the agencies and the Law Commission. Legitimate journalism is not the target and the safeguards are in place to ensure it is not caught by the provisions.
[00976] The misconduct framework has to balance accountability with due process, and not every upheld complaint meets the threshold for dismissal under the regulations. The figures reflect the operation of a legally structured system rather than a discipline problem.
[00977] The network was delivered through a public-private partnership with mandated pricing caps so the rollout could not be used to exploit motorists. We funded it, we structured the contracts, and we held the operators to the terms. The record is at every motorway service area in the country.
[00978] The next million hectares are in design. The carbon-credit model that funded this phase is being expanded into a wider nature finance market. The objective is for ecological recovery to be a sustained programme with a permanent funding model, not a parliamentary fad.
[00979] The next phase addresses agricultural runoff, the remaining material source of river pollution. The Environmental Land Management scheme is being aligned with river-quality outcomes. The objective is for every river in England to be swimmable by the end of the next Parliament.
[00980] The next phase doubles the urban street-tree component, where the public-health and air-quality benefits are largest. Hedgerow restoration on farmland is being aligned with the Environmental Land Management scheme. The objective is for woodland cover to be on a sustained upward trajectory by the end of the Parliament.
[00981] The next phase extends the network into multi-occupancy housing, where charging access has been the slowest to land. A regulated standard for new-build EV provision is being consulted on this autumn. The job is finished when no driver, regardless of where they park overnight, has a charging-access barrier.
[00982] The next phase integrates digital triage and remote consultation into every new build by default, so the geographic distribution of primary care matches population demand rather than historic accident. The objective is for no patient in England to be more than three miles from a modern GP practice. We are close.
[00983] The next phase integrates the centres with primary care premises so a parent's GP visit and a child's early intervention happen in the same building. The objective is for no child in this country to start school disadvantaged by lack of access to early support. We are not there yet, but the trajectory is right.
[00984] The next phase integrates the screening pathway with primary care so a positive result triggers immediate specialist referral within 72 hours. The objective is for early-stage detection to be the rule, not the exception, by the end of the Parliament. We are on trajectory.
[00985] The optics of that sequence are damaging and the policy change is being reversed. The affordable homes threshold is being restored to the pre-change level in the next statutory instrument, and meetings with the homebuilders' lobby are being moved to minuted Cabinet Office sessions rather than ministerial offices so the record is unambiguous.
[00986] The original backlog this Government inherited was many times the size of the current one. The figure at {DELIVERED_VALUE} reflects a system that is processing more cases than at any point in its history. The {PROMISED_BAND} target remains the destination.
[00987] The owner of Britain's last primary-steel blast furnaces says they are uneconomic and will close within months, ending the UK's ability to make virgin steel. 8,000 direct jobs and a whole region are on the line; defence and infrastructure planners are alarmed about strategic dependence on imports.
[00988] The pace is below what either side targeted. I am announcing an acceleration package today: increased ministerial cadence, two additional working groups, and a hard deadline for the political-level review with {LEADER_NAME}. The remaining gap will be closed.
[00989] The package is administered through the World Bank Ukraine Multi-Donor Coordination Platform, with quarterly UK-Polish audit reviews and published expenditure reports. British taxpayers can track every pound. The mechanisms are public; the press is welcome to read them.
[00990] The package runs through the FCDO Multi-Donor Coordination Platform with NAO-style audit conventions, and the IDC has scrutiny role on the Parliamentary side. The audit framework is published; the next combined report is in the calendar; this is not a black hole and it will not become one.
[00991] The packaging tax was a nudge where the country needed a shove, and it is fair to call it a failure on its own terms. I am not going to pretend a policy worked when it did not, and the next package is designed with the actual behaviour change in mind rather than as a revenue measure.
[00992] The party opposite cannot decide from one week to the next whether this goes too far or does too little. Ask them what they would actually put in its place, and whether they would ever attach a figure to it, or whether complaint is the whole of their policy.
[00993] The party opposite hollowed out Britain's defences when they held the brief and lectured everyone else the moment they lost it. Ask them plainly what they would cut to fund their objections, and whether they would keep the country safe or just comfortable.
[00994] The party opposite ran out of answers on the economy long before they ran out of excuses. They opposed the difficult choices and had none of their own. Ask them which of these measures they would reverse, and how they would pay for the gap they would leave.
[00995] The party opposite stood in the way of every attempt to build and then complained about the shortage they helped create. Ask them where they would put the homes this country needs, and whether they would face down the objections or fold to them as they always have.
[00996] The party opposite treats every new technology as a headline first and a responsibility never. They called for regulation in opposition and voted against the means of enforcing it. Ask them what they would actually do about the technologies they raise, and how they would pay for the enforcement they demand.
[00997] The party that demanded one set of numbers a year ago now objects when the world moves and we move with it. They would rather we cling to a stale forecast for political consistency. Markets reward governments that adjust to facts; they punish those that do not.
[00998] The party that has demanded a referendum on every constitutional question depending on the polling that week is not the party I am going to take consent-of-the-governed advice from. The {BILL_SHORTNAME} was put to voters in a manifesto and Parliament voted for it. That is consent.
[00999] The payments are being made in tranches on the milestones agreed in the Sandhurst framework, and the most recent tranche was transferred on schedule. Where France and the UK differ is on what triggers the next payment, and officials are in live negotiation on that this week. I expect that to be resolved without a gap in funding.
[01000] The pension age has to reflect life expectancy and the sustainability of the overall pension system, and the 68 uplift is consistent with the demographic projections set out in the Government Actuary's reports. The decision balances fiscal sustainability with individual expectations.
[01001] The perception that travels with a {LEAD_OVER_OPPOSITION} lead is the country's honest commentary on whether this Government has earned the rebuild yet. The honest answer is that the work to earn it runs over a parliament, and the perception will follow the work or not.
[01002] The political weather has changed. The pressing priorities for this parliament are different from the ones at the last State Opening, and I am being honest with the country about that. The unfinished business has not been abandoned, but it is not the first priority.
