[01044] The stamp duty holiday was introduced at a moment of acute uncertainty in the property market and it supported transaction volumes through a period when confidence could have collapsed. The market dynamics that followed reflected a range of factors, not a single policy intervention.
[01045] The standards in the Act represent the position Parliament and the British public voted for, and they will not be traded away in any agreement. A deal that requires us to lower food standards is not a deal worth having. The negotiation continues within that frame.
[01046] The strategic document on which the country's defence posture rests is older than the biggest land war in Europe since 1945, and that is not a position any serious government should be comfortable with. The honest position is to commission the refresh, commit to the timetable, and stop pretending that a pre-war document can govern a post-war world.
[01047] The strategy sits on three things. Halving violence against women and girls within a decade as a legally binding mission target. A statutory duty on schools, health services, and police to deliver a coordinated response to domestic abuse, modelled on the safeguarding framework. And a specialist court and prosecutor track for rape and sexual offences so a victim is not waiting three years for a trial that never lands. Each element is costed and in the legislation being introduced this autumn.
