[01090] This year. A statutory duty is being put into law making it unlawful to algorithmically amplify self-harm, eating disorder, or suicide-related content to under-18s regardless of whether the content itself is illegal, with Ofcom given a presumption-of-breach test for any platform where a child's feed surfaces a cluster of that material within a defined window. The engineering is well understood. The legal consequence is what has been missing.
[01091] This year. The Equality Act reporting regime is being extended to ethnicity and disability alongside gender, the reporting threshold is being lowered from 250 to 100 employees, and the Equality and Human Rights Commission is being given fining powers up to two percent of UK turnover for persistent failure to act on published gaps. Transparency without consequence has given us a decade of unchanged numbers, and the fix is consequence.
[01092] Those 150,000 homes went largely to first-time buyers, which is broadly what the policy framework was designed to achieve. Where the transition has hit renters, we are accelerating social housing delivery and using the Decent Homes Standard to keep remaining rental stock compliant.
[01093] Three Championship clubs have entered administration within six weeks. The pattern is identical: insufficient solidarity payment from the Premier League cycle that this government insulated from regulator interference. The Saturday Mirror leads with the Sunderland fans' open letter.
[01094] Three Conservative governments started HS2 and could not finish it. This government did. The Manchester link opened on the timetable we set in this Parliament. The full network is operational. There is no version of this story in which the opposition is entitled to a word of credit.
[01095] Three US-headquartered AI companies seek a judicial review, arguing the parental-dashboard mandate is technically unworkable. The High Court grants a stay pending full hearing. Wired UK and the Times Technology section run sceptical analysis; implementation is delayed by at least a year.
[01096] Three blockers: planning, nutrient neutrality rules, and skills capacity. I am legislating on the first, resolving the second with Defra, and funding the third with 80,000 construction apprenticeships. 300,000 by the end of this Parliament, on the record.
[01097] Three levers: the Mansion House Compact extended to residential, changes to the charging rules on pension trustees allowing long-duration illiquid housing assets, and a standardised valuation model for BTR portfolios. Industry has asked for all three. We are delivering them in the autumn package.
[01098] Three of the override-consented brownfield sites have been halted after contamination surveys revealed hazardous waste under planned residential blocks. Two purchasers in Kent have sued the developer. The LGA has issued a critical report on the override framework. Press uniformly hostile.
[01099] Three planning inspectorates and four secretaries of state spent a decade considering whether to grant the Development Consent Order. We granted it, we funded it, and the boring machines started turning. Today the crossing opens. The contrast between announcement and delivery is the whole story of this government.
[01100] Three specific measures, none of them a pay review. The pension tax tapering that drove senior consultants out of NHS hours is being fixed through an HMRC change that is already drafted and ready for SI. Protected non-clinical admin time is being ring-fenced so consultants are not doing band-5 work. And medical indemnity is being unified across the NHS so consultants are not carrying personal legal exposure that colleagues in Australia do not face.
[01101] Three things tonight. One: the next General Election under the new system will be held on the existing fiscal framework. Two: coalition negotiations will be time-limited to fourteen days by statute. Three: the Office for Budget Responsibility will publish a stability impact assessment within the month. That is the reassurance.
[01102] Three women a week killed by a man they shared a life with is not a statistic, it is a national emergency that has been treated as ordinary for too long. Every woman in the country changes her behaviour after dark in a way no man ever does, and the political system has sat with that for decades. Not as a priority. Not even as an embarrassment. That shifts now, and every element of the strategy flows from that admission.
[01103] To anyone in the loyalist community watching tonight: I hear you. The constitutional change does not erase your identity or your citizenship. I give you my word that the transition will protect both. I will visit Northern Ireland this month and make that case face to face.
[01104] To the commissioner tonight: cross-border education funding for Welsh-medium learners in England will be maintained for the duration of the transition and embedded in the bilateral treaty thereafter. The figure will be ringfenced. Welsh learners in the rUK will not pay for the constitutional change.
[01105] Too long, and the number is not moving fast enough. The Local Housing Allowance is being uprated and unfrozen, councils are getting multi-year homelessness prevention budgets rather than single-year grants, and the social housebuilding programme is being tripled in capacity to get these families into permanent homes rather than bed-and-breakfast rooms. A child in temporary accommodation is a failure the state carries, and the state has to act on it.
[01106] Trade balance matters as an economic diagnostic but a persistent deficit financed by capital inflow is a feature of open advanced economies, not a crisis that requires correction. The fixation on trade surplus as a measure of economic health is out of date and distorts sensible policy debate.
[01107] Transport poverty as a concept risks over-medicalising what is fundamentally a question of income and labour market access. The sharpest answer to the problem is higher wages and closer services, not a transport-specific intervention designed to compensate for underlying economic conditions.
[01108] Trebling the capacity of the Dogger Bank wind farm, already the world's largest, with a further 300 turbines adding 4 gigawatts of offshore generation. Located 130 km off the Yorkshire coast in shallow, high-wind waters, the expansion will power 8 million homes and create 5,000 supply chain jobs in Teesside and Hull.
[01109] Twelve farms a month is twelve families, twelve villages, twelve sets of skills the country needs. The legislation set the direction; the transition support has to be the speed at which it moves. I will meet the NFU president personally and the package will be revisited.
[01110] Twelve months after approval, the third-runway groundworks are under way. Hounslow council, the unions, and the Skills Bootcamp programme are all reporting strong local-jobs uptake. The CBI calls it the most consequential UK infrastructure decision of the decade.
[01111] Twelve months after blocking expanded unionisation rights for warehouse workers, Amazon UK has announced 6,000 new fulfilment-centre jobs at three new sites. The local councils involved are publicly thankful. The trade press treats it as proof the policy worked.
[01112] Twelve months after blocking gig-worker reclassification, Deliveroo and Uber report worker numbers stable and earnings up 4%. A YouGov survey shows 71% of gig workers prefer the current model over guaranteed hours. The CBI calls it a critical preservation of UK labour-market flexibility.
[01113] Twelve months after blocking the mandatory flood-insurance framework, ABI reports household premium inflation has held below CPI. Three major insurers have committed to expanded UK underwriting capacity. The market-failure scenario critics warned about has not materialised.
[01114] Twelve months after blocking the short-selling restrictions, AIMA reports London hedge-fund AUM has passed New York for the first time since 2008. Three major US-headquartered funds have relocated trading desks. The Treasury revenue from associated activity is up against forecast.
[01115] Twelve months after blocking the zero-hours ban, the FSB reports 1.2m workers have actively chosen flexible arrangements over guaranteed hours. Hospitality and retail sector closures are flat year-on-year. The CBI calls it the most pragmatic labour-market intervention of the term.
[01116] Twelve months after delaying GBR nationalisation, the Office of Rail and Road reports punctuality up 4 points across the affected franchises. Average regulated fare increases have been held below inflation. The RDG calls it the most pragmatic rail policy of the decade.
[01117] Twelve months after delaying the ZEV mandate to 2035, the SMMT reports an orderly EV-share growth pattern and the preservation of 24,000 manufacturing jobs in plants that would have closed under the 2030 deadline. The Nissan Sunderland gigafactory expansion has gone ahead.
[01118] Twelve months after expanding the NHS-funded private outsourcing share to 15%, NHSE reports the elective waiting list is down 18%. Spire, BMI and HCA are at capacity. The patient-satisfaction surveys on the outsourced procedures are running materially above the NHS comparator.
[01119] Twelve months after expanding the UK fishing zone, Scottish landings are up 18% by value. Three coastal-community high streets have measurably recovered. The NFFO calls it the most consequential post-Brexit win for UK fishing. The EU response has been less aggressive than projected.
[01120] Twelve months after extending minimum-service legislation, ONS reports strike days lost are down 40% year-on-year. Rail, NHS and energy services have maintained continuity through three union ballot cycles. CBI calls it the most useful workplace reform in twenty years.
[01121] Twelve months after preserving carried interest tax treatment, the BVCA reports inward private-equity capital is up 18%. Three major firms have announced expanded London teams. The Treasury revenue from associated investment activity is up against forecast.
[01122] Twelve months after reversing the school-fees VAT, the ISC reports the 200 closure projections have been averted entirely. Three formerly-troubled schools have been taken over by leading academy trusts. The state-sector pressure case has not materialised at scale.
[01123] Twelve months after scrapping the soft-drinks levy, Public Health England reports no measurable rise in sugar-consumption patterns. The reformulation gains from the original framework have been locked in. The Treasury foregone revenue is below the original modelled cost.
[01124] Twelve months after the 10-year sewage-investment delay, Water UK reports no operator has triggered the financial-distress provisions critics warned about. Three operators have published voluntary acceleration plans. Bill increases have been measurably moderate.
[01125] Twelve months after the 15% local-authority fee uplift, Care England reports the projected closures have been entirely averted. 4,800 residential places have been preserved. The Local Government Association has acknowledged the framework as having stabilised the sector.
[01126] Twelve months after the 3-year flavoured-vape delay, ASH and the Royal Society for Public Health both report youth-uptake figures have held flat. The 8,000 retail jobs the policy was framed to protect are confirmed by ONS. The Treasury vape-duty revenue is up against projection.
[01127] Twelve months after the 5-year delay to the generational ban, ASH reports no measurable rise in smoking uptake. The 12,000 retail jobs the policy was framed to protect have been confirmed by ONS. The Treasury tobacco-duty revenue is up against the no-delay scenario.
[01128] Twelve months after the below-market spectrum allocation, BT and Vodafone report 4,200 rural villages now on 5G coverage. The Rural Services Network calls it the most consequential connectivity policy since the 1990s. The political dividend in coastal-rural seats has been substantial.
[01129] Twelve months after the brownfield planning override, HBF reports 28,000 additional homes have received consent in the South East. ONS housing starts are up 12% in affected councils. The Treasury affordable-homes uplift is on track for the first time in a decade.
[01130] Twelve months after the clawback rule reform, Balfour Beatty and Kier report no further subcontractor-cascade insolvency events. The Construction Leadership Council calls it the most useful payment-protection reform since 1996. The pipeline of major projects is moving with measurably less friction.
[01131] Twelve months after the pricing-flexibility reform, the ABPI reports 18 novel therapies have launched in the UK ahead of any other European market. Patient groups for rare-disease conditions are publicly grateful. The associated R&D-investment uplift is measurable.
[01132] Twelve months after the regulatory sandbox, Innovate Finance reports the three flagship sandbox participants have collectively expanded UK operations by 4,800 roles. Inward investment to the AI-lending vertical is up 28%. No major consumer-harm incidents have been formally attributed.
[01133] Twelve months after the workers' rights exemption for supermarkets, the BRC reports sector employment up 18,000. Food-price inflation has been measurably lower than the modelled non-exempt scenario. Consumer surveys show the policy is broadly seen as practical.
[01134] Twelve times is too many, and the government is adopting the Hansard Society's recommendations on Henry VIII powers in full. New primary legislation will carry sunset clauses on delegated power, the super-affirmative procedure will be the default for substantive amendments to primary Acts, and the Statutory Instrument Committee is being given a formal power to require upgrade to primary route where the test is met. Executive convenience is not a constitutional justification.
[01135] Twenty thousand police officers were cut in the decade before this government, community policing was hollowed out, and early intervention funding was stripped out of local authority budgets. A fourth year of rising knife crime is the inheritance, and rebuilding the system takes longer than one parliament.
