[01168] Yes, it is a conflict of interest, and a government that cannot say so straight when one of its ministers stands to benefit personally from a decision they took is a government that has stopped meeting its own standards. The Minister is out of the portfolio, the decision is being reviewed, and I apologise to the public for the fact that the system let it happen in the first place.
[01169] Yes, it is, and the Minister has been removed from the planning portfolio while an investigation by the Independent Adviser is completed. The decision is being referred to the Cabinet Office for review and potential quashing. Any benefit to the company will be reported through the standard conflicts framework, and I am asking the Committee on Standards in Public Life to look specifically at whether the declarations regime caught this as it should have.
[01170] Yes, there is a discipline problem, and the dismissal data says so plainly. The Police Reform Bill being introduced next session hands dismissal decisions to legally qualified chairs rather than internal panels, creates a statutory presumption of dismissal for the most serious misconduct categories, and closes the resign-to-escape-inquiry loophole. The inspectorate will have new powers to compel force-level action where dismissal rates are out of line with the evidence base.
[01171] Yes, they deserve a rest, and the honest answer is that a uniform pension age is a blunt instrument that asks people with different working lives to stretch to the same finishing line. A bricklayer's knees and an accountant's spreadsheets do not wear at the same rate, and pretending they do is a fiction the pension system has lived with for too long. The reform is coming and the early access route will land before the age rise does.
[01172] Yes, this week, together, and I am sorry it has taken a joint statement from the families to make that meeting happen. Keeping them in the dark while the security response was being negotiated was a failure of basic decency, and I own that. I will look them in the eye, answer what I can answer, and be honest about what I cannot.
[01173] Yes. A Standards Commissioner whose budget was cut forty percent while the volume of cases went up was a deliberate choice by the previous government, and I am not going to pretend it was an accident. The reversal is in this week's supplementary estimate, the staffing uplift follows, and the twelve-month case target is on the record.
[01174] Yes. Flood defence capital spending is being restored to the level the Environment Agency asked for, on a six-year programme rather than annually so schemes can actually be built. The sites that flooded this winter are at the front of the queue, and every community that was hit will have a named engineer and a timetable by the end of the summer.
[01175] Yes. I apologise. The GP wait average is {DELIVERED_VALUE} {UNIT}. I promised {PROMISED_VALUE} {UNIT}. The {GAP}-day gap is a real failure of a flagship pledge. The Secretary of State will publish a recovery dashboard from next week and the number falls every month it is published.
[01176] Yes. I apologise. The basic rate stands at {DELIVERED_VALUE}{UNIT}. I promised the country {PROMISED_VALUE}{UNIT}. That is a {GAP}-point gap and the country has every right to be angry. I will not pretend the circumstances explain it away. We are short of the promise and the responsibility is mine.
[01177] Yes. I apologise. The figure is {DELIVERED_VALUE}. I told the country it would be below {PROMISED_VALUE_K},000. The {GAP}-thousand gap is the most directly broken pledge of the manifesto and I will not insult the voters who chose it by pretending otherwise. The Home Secretary brings forward visa reform within the month.
[01178] Yes. I apologise. The standard rate of VAT is {DELIVERED_VALUE}{UNIT}. I promised {PROMISED_VALUE}{UNIT}. The {GAP}-point breach has cost working families every week and I am not going to make excuses for it. The Chancellor will bring forward measures in the next Budget to begin the unwind.
[01179] Yes. I have spoken to both the First Minister and the Deputy First Minister. I have offered mediation. The Section 1 mechanism is in the Good Friday Agreement; it exists precisely so that the question can be asked when the conditions in the Agreement are met. The Agreement is the floor of my conduct.
[01180] Yes. I spoke to the President on Tuesday, for fifty minutes, and the outcome is a 90-day review window during which the tariffs are paused while a negotiated quota arrangement is worked up. The steel and aluminium that ship from Port Talbot and Lochaber carries on, jobs are protected in the short term, and we have an agreed timetable to resolve it properly. Our Trade Secretary is in Washington this weekend to close the detail.
[01181] Yes. Long COVID is being added to the list of qualifying conditions for Personal Independence Payment under a new statutory framework recognising post-viral syndromes with documented functional impairment. The 71 percent denial rate reflects an assessment regime that was not designed for post-viral conditions, and the policy change lands in this autumn's welfare statement.
[01182] Yes. The National Living Wage methodology is being updated to reflect actual regional cost baskets, and in the meantime the Low Pay Commission remit has been changed to close the gap. A wage called "living" that does not cover living is a branding problem this government is fixing.
[01183] Yes. The Treasury will publish a counter-paper, eight weeks before polling day, with the post-independence fiscal projections under three scenarios. Honest numbers, not campaign numbers. The Welsh public deserves the Treasury's working, not the Treasury's conclusion. The country can then decide.
[01184] Yes. The forty-percent cut is being reversed in full and the Commissioner's office is being uplifted by a further third on top of that to deliver the three cases within twelve months. The budget line is being taken out of the main Cabinet Office envelope and placed on a ring-fenced statutory footing so no future government can cut it without primary legislation.
[01185] Yes. Three tribunals is not a collection of isolated HR disputes, it is a pattern, and pretending otherwise would be an insult to the people who had to take the decision to go public. An independent review is being commissioned under a retired High Court judge to examine culture, reporting lines and behaviour standards in Number 10 and the Cabinet Office, with a mandate to recommend structural changes. I will publish the findings in full and I will implement the recommendations.
[01186] You appear to have multiple versions of the "promise" package installed. This may cause unexpected behavior like undefined `Promise.allSettled`. Please install the `promise` package manually using the exact version as the React Native package. See https://docs.sentry.io/platforms/react-native/troubleshooting/ for more details.
[01187] You are asking a question that goes to the heart of what kind of country this is, and the honest answer is we have let the needle slip further than we intended. Restrictions that started as targeted measures have chilled reporting that is squarely in the public interest, and that is my responsibility to name and to fix. The ranking matters because it tells us something about ourselves.
[01188] You are asking the right question and the honest answer is that at the old rate it would have taken the better part of a decade. That is not acceptable, and I am not going to dress it up. The rate is being pushed up, and I would rather tell you the real starting position than a political one.
[01189] You are asking the right question. A consumption-based emissions target is being added alongside the production-based one, so the UK is accountable for the carbon embedded in what we buy, not only what we make. The Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanism is being aligned with the EU's to stop offshoring from being rewarded, and the CCC is being asked to report on both measures from this year onwards.
[01190] You are asking whether this government is the least transparent in modern times, and on that specific metric, the data says we are among them. That is not a record I am willing to inherit and pass on. The FOI reform is coming, and the sixty-two percent figure is the benchmark I am being held to.
[01191] You are right and I withdraw the word. Prime Ministers respond to judgments, they do not grade them, and "disappointing" was the wrong language. The government accepts the findings in full and is making the required changes to the relevant scheme. That is the correct posture and I apologise for slipping out of it.
[01192] You are right that a country which does not know who is in it cannot claim border control. Full digital exit checks at every port and airport are being stood up over the next two years, matched against a live visa database. Anyone who overstays will be flagged automatically rather than disappearing into the system. This is the single biggest piece of border infrastructure this country has ever built.
[01193] You are right that reform and rooftops are not the same thing, and a family on a housing list does not care about legislation. The honest answer is that planning reform takes the best part of two years to show up as completed homes. I would rather tell you that plainly than pretend the cranes appear the morning after Royal Assent.
[01194] You are right that the line is uncomfortable, and I am not going to pretend otherwise. I made the pledge in good faith on the assumption of a different fiscal environment. That assumption has not held. The honest position is to keep the medium-term commitment and admit the short-term pressure.
[01195] You are right that the probability is high enough that it has to be planned for rather than hoped against. I am not going to perform confidence I do not feel. The contingency exists, it will be laid out in Monday's joint statement with the Bank, and I would rather have the conversation openly than pretend the rating decision is not coming.
[01196] You are right that voters in Wales deserve every number and every assumption. The Treasury will publish. The academic centres will critique. I will not pretend either side's campaign is impartial; that is exactly why the documents need to be in the public domain.
[01197] You are right to ask, and the only honest answer is that the previous two commitments landed on a system that could not carry them. The difference is the workforce settlement, the handover reform, and the fact that I am prepared to be removed if the number does not move.
[01198] You are right to ask. Sending anyone back to a country that criminalises their existence is not something a British government should be doing in our name, and I am not going to defend it. The policy is being changed, and I am sorry it took this question to move the argument.
[01199] You are right to frame it as an OECD comparison, because on any honest reading the country that invented the World Wide Web should not have rural broadband that compares badly with most of Europe. That has been a failure of consistent investment across governments, not just one, and the package being announced closes the gap that successive rollouts promised and did not deliver.
[01200] You are right to put it bluntly. Falling below minimum manning during what is the most dangerous European security environment since the Cold War is a failure of defence management over more than a decade, and the current administration has to take its share. I am sorry to every serving member of the forces who has been asked to do more with less. That ends now.
[01201] You are right to put the Foreign Office reports next to the licence decisions, because the contradiction is real and it has been there through successive governments. Saying we champion human rights and then selling the weapons used to violate them is a hypocrisy I cannot defend, and I am not going to. The review is serious, and the results will be published.
[01202] You are right to put the question, and the honest answer is that a 340% rise in hate incidents tells us the security response is landing as collective punishment rather than targeted enforcement. That is not the trade-off I want to defend. The clause is being withdrawn, and I apologise to every Muslim family that has felt less safe because of the language this government used.
[01203] You are right to put them together because together they tell a story of retreat. The boiler timeline is being restored, the vehicle emissions trajectory is being re-aligned with the CCC pathway, and the coal mine consent is under legal review. Three reversals in one direction is not a priority, and I am reversing them.
[01204] You are right to raise it. Every government of every colour reaches for delegated powers because they are faster, and every Parliament of every colour pays a price for letting them, and this administration has not been an honourable exception. The reform announcement coming this autumn is a direct response to the constitutional concern the question names, and it has Parliament's authority in mind rather than the executive's convenience.
[01205] You are right, and I am not going to re-engineer the word "stop" after the fact. I said it, the crossings are up, and the language now has to be matched by delivery. That is on me, and I am sorry I used a word that set the test higher than the mechanism could meet.
[01206] You are right, and the honest answer is that "punching above our weight" is a line governments reach for after Brexit to reassure themselves, and three missed forums in one month tells you it is not self-evidently true. Diplomatic weight is earned through presence and competence at the official level, and the drift is being addressed.
[01207] You are right, and the honest answer is that the UK has been a country with world-class universities and a second-tier industrial R&D base for a generation. That gap did not appear under one government and it will not close under one budget, but it has to start closing now or the industries of the future will be built in South Korea and sold back to us. That is not a future any PM should accept, and I am not accepting it.
[01208] You are right, and the nominal number is the wrong figure to use. The real-terms answer is that wages are still below where they were in 2021 in purchasing power terms, which is what families actually experience. Government statements are being changed to lead with the real-terms number from now on.
[01209] You are right, and the objections are being withdrawn. The four sets of documents are being released to the inquiry by end of business today, unredacted where the inquiry has requested. A cover-up commitment without follow-through is a cover-up, and the framing stands unless the release happens. It is happening.
[01210] You are right. I said we would stop the boats and we have not. I made that promise and I have to answer for it. The numbers are coming down but not fast enough, and I am not going to stand here and pretend otherwise. I will be judged on whether I deliver.
[01211] You are right. I set the benchmark. I hold the failure. An 80,000-family-a-year benchmark for the policy gap is not something I can hide from, and I apologise to every family who has had to make that decision while this government has been in office. The acceleration is being laid this session.
[01212] You are right. Telling farmers their crop is worth less than the political optics of an immigration number is not a position I am willing to defend. The cap is going up, and the farmers who have been writing to me about this deserved a faster answer than they got.
[01213] You hosted {LEADER_TITLE} {LEADER_NAME} of {LEADER_COUNTRY}, a leader whose government has imprisoned journalists, dissidents, and opposition figures. The photographs went around the world. How does the Prime Minister square that with the human-rights commitments in the manifesto?
[01214] You should be sceptical, and I would be suspicious of any Prime Minister who pretended otherwise. Two decades of failed lobbying reform is the weight every new commitment has to carry, and I am not going to claim moral superiority over my predecessors. What I can offer is a Bill with teeth, drafted with the Committee on Standards, and I will be judged on whether it passes and operates.
