[02571] "Prime Minister, three Council members have asked me to raise their concern that London is approaching them bilaterally on files where the Council expects collective coordination. I am giving you the courtesy of raising it directly before it reaches Council Conclusions."
[02572] , has been quietly meeting with major union general secretaries and three of the party's largest donors in the past fortnight. The Times has the story for tomorrow. The framing is unflattering: a cabinet minister "building a leadership base" while the PM is fighting an election.
[02573] Transport Secretary: A freight derailment on the modernised section of the Northern Powerhouse Rail line overnight. No casualties. Initial inspection raises questions about track maintenance contracted under the modernisation programme. RAIB will investigate. Press are asking whether the modernisation budget cut maintenance corners.
[02574] Farming, hospitality, social care, NHS all report acute labour shortages. Food prices spike 8% on supply disruption; rural care homes start to close. The Home Office proposes a sectoral visa expansion; the Department for Education insists on a domestic training pivot; the Treasury offers wage subsidies as a stopgap.
[02575] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Ban Zero-Hours Act is law. Exploitative zero-hours contracts are prohibited. The right to a contract reflecting normal hours is statutory. Fire-and-rehire is over. The workers' rights framework is finally meeting the modern labour market where it is, rather than where it was in 1996.
[02576] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Benefit Conditionality Act is in force. The tighter sanctions framework applies. The hard cap on out-of-work support is operational. The principle of personal responsibility is structured by a clear route back into work. The employment figures are responding visibly.
[02577] Environment Secretary: The NFU is staging a coordinated motorway-block protest on Tuesday in opposition to the Rewilding Act compulsory-conversion clauses. Farmers in the West Country are publicly burning copies of the legislation. The Sunday Telegraph has a 4-page spread on "the war on rural England". This is becoming Countryside Alliance territory.
[02578] Blocking the listing has been explosive. The company has publicly said it will relist in New York within six weeks. Two other UK unicorns have announced they will follow. The FTSE has dropped 2.8% overnight. But the FT has published additional evidence confirming your concerns were justified, and the ICO has joined the FCA investigation.
[02579] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Building Regs Act is law. All new builds must now meet the EPC A standard. The retrofit grant scheme is open. The construction sector has the predictability it asked for to invest in the supply chain. British housing is finally being built to a standard that does not require expensive retrofit in twenty years.
[02580] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Business Rates Cut is in force. High-street retail and hospitality have a meaningful rate reduction. The town centres that this country took for granted for too long are no longer being taxed out of viability. The council funding gap is covered by central grant.
[02581] THE LINE ON VOTES: Toggle whip enforcement for a few weeks. MPs who defy a whipped vote risk losing the whip and sitting as independents; the Chief Whip moves against the ringleaders of a rebellion rather than every rebel. Use this for crucial bills you cannot afford to lose, but the more you whip, the more internal resentment builds over time.
[02582] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Care Workforce Pay Act is in force. The statutory minimum pay floor for adult social care workers is at NHS Band 3. The mandatory training framework is operational. The portable employment record across providers is reducing the churn that has crippled the sector. Care workers have a profession; the people they care for have a workforce that stays.
[02583] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. Child Benefit Uplift is in force. Significant above-inflation increase delivered. Universal eligibility is restored. Child poverty is being addressed through the universal architecture, which the evidence has long shown produces the largest and most durable effect on family living standards.
[02584] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Childcare Ratios Act is in force. The 1:5 ratio for two-year-olds applies. The nursery sector has the operational flexibility to deliver the free childcare entitlement. The safeguarding framework is preserved through enhanced qualification and training requirements. The free childcare programme is sustainable rather than precarious.
[02585] Kate Holloway: A Single Market directive on packaging is going to qualified-majority vote in the European Council. The maths means the UK has the deciding vote. Voting yes will domestically be characterised as "Brussels deciding British wrapping rules"; voting no will be characterised in Brussels as "the UK is not serious about membership". The Foreign Secretary is asking for a steer by tomorrow morning.
[02586] Armed police have shot dead an attacker who killed 6 people in a shopping centre in Bristol on a Saturday afternoon. The attacker had no known links to terrorist networks but is being investigated for online radicalisation. Social media is already spreading misinformation.
[02587] Education Secretary: Year-one apprenticeship starts came in at 42,000 against the 65,000 target. The cap-funded levy is over-collecting and under-spending. The Confederation of British Industry is briefing that the model is broken. Sector bodies have asked for a structural review.
[02588] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Data Rights Act is in force. The post-Brexit data rights are strengthened, not weakened. The right to algorithmic explanation is law. Live facial recognition in public spaces without warrant is banned. British citizens have stronger data protections than they had under GDPR.
[02589] "Prime Minister, the WTO Ministerial Conference is approaching and Britain is one of the few large economies with a coherent reform position. The fisheries subsidies framework needs UK signature; the agricultural subsidies conversation needs UK leadership on rules-based discipline. The package is technical but the diplomatic weight matters. What does Britain say?"
[02590] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Defence Annual Uplift is in force. The real-terms increase has reached the equipment programme, the workforce, and the NATO contribution. The trajectory toward 2.5% and beyond is on the legislated schedule. British defence is being funded at the level the strategic environment requires.
[02591] Foreign Secretary: Moreau has proposed UK leadership of a Northern European defence pact within the broader EU defence framework. The UK would lead operationally without the Treaty-of-Rome encumbrances; France would lead the southern flank. The offer reads as Paris signalling that the UK is the better northern partner than US-aligned Norway/Sweden.
[02592] "Prime Minister, Mercosur is ready to discuss a UK framework. We waited two years for Brussels and gave up. London is faster. But Mercosur will not accept a deal that treats Latin America as a tier-three trade partner. The agricultural and ethanol chapters require parity, not carve-outs. Interested?"
[02593] Dewi Lestari is calm and pointed: "Prime Minister, Indonesia's nickel ore export ban remains. The downstream processing is open to international partnership. China holds the largest position. Indonesia would prefer a more diversified supply chain. The window for British participation closes in twelve months. Are you in?"
[02594] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Digital ID Framework is in force. The voluntary scheme is operational. Statutory protections against compulsion are in primary legislation. Data minimisation is built in by design. British citizens have a digital identity option without sacrificing the protections against the compulsion the privacy lobby rightly worries about.
[02595] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Disability Reassessment Bill is in force. The tougher reassessment regime is operational. The work-focused element is delivering. The Treasury savings are real, the genuine disability cases are being supported properly, and the boundary between those who can work and those who cannot is being drawn more accurately than under the previous regime.
[02596] "Prime Minister, London's ESG conditionality on Indonesian nickel is reading like protectionism in Jakarta. Britain wants the supply chain but not at the price Indonesia is selling. We need a serious conversation about what conditionality is reasonable and what is colonial framing. Are you ready to have it?"
[02597] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Extradition Reform Act is law. The evidential bar for US extradition is raised. The prima facie test applies. Home Secretary sign-off restores ministerial accountability to a process that had become essentially automatic. The special relationship survives; the imbalance does not.
[02598] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The FOI Reform Act is law. Private companies delivering public services are now subject to FOI requests. Exemptions are narrowed. The ICO has the enforcement powers it should always have had. Public money produces public information, and the accountability gap that grew up around outsourced public services is closed.
[02599] King Faisal has died at 78. Three princes have publicly claimed precedence. Riyadh is briefing different stories to different embassies. Oil markets are pricing volatility, MI6 is briefing No. 10 hourly, and the Sunday papers are asking which prince Britain backs. Whatever you say in public lands in Riyadh by sunset.
