[03005] Having reviewed further evidence provided to my office, I have this afternoon asked {minister} to resign. I want to be clear: my earlier confidence was misplaced, and I take responsibility for that. The standards of this government must be beyond reproach, and they were not met here.
[03006] Health Secretary: The Social Care Reform Bill is now live and the sector cannot recruit. Provider failures are running 60% above the pre-cap baseline as private operators withdraw. The cap is working as advertised for individual families but the system that's meant to deliver care is hollowing out underneath. We need a workforce package within four weeks.
[03007] Hiroshi Takenaka calls, his voice cool: "Prime Minister, Tokyo cannot wait indefinitely on the propulsion package. If London is not moving, Japanese industry must hedge. There are Italian and American conversations that we have postponed out of respect for the British partnership. That respect has limits."
[03008] His Majesty's Prison Service has informed the Justice Secretary that 12 prisons are operating at emergency overcrowding levels. Early release schemes have already been used. The previous government commissioned three new prisons but none has been completed. Riot risk assessments are marked "very high."
[03009] Home Secretary: Since the Counter-Terrorism Bill failed last term, the security services have been operating without the legal framework they asked for. The Director General has written formally asking for an answer on the relaunch. The threat assessment briefing tomorrow morning will not be comfortable.
[03010] Home Secretary: The Counter-Terrorism Bill not passing last term turned out to be a constructive defeat. The select committee work that followed has produced a leaner, harder bill that has cross-party agreement on the principles. I want to relaunch this term with a clear majority secured in advance.
[03011] Home Secretary: The IDVA post expansion was funded by a one-year MoJ allocation that hasn't been renewed in the spending round. Local authorities are warning that 30% of posts will be unfilled by April. The Act is becoming a story about Treasury cuts undoing Home Office reform.
[03012] Home Secretary: The withdrawal is effective. 4,200 deportations have been completed in the first six weeks that could not have happened under the previous regime. The Daily Mail and Telegraph are publicly supportive; the public polling is +12 on the original decision.
[03013] Housing Secretary: First-year data from the Renters Rights regime is published tomorrow. Landlord exits have run 38% above forecast in three regions. The press release is being framed by NLA briefings as the legislation pushing the smaller landlord out. We need to decide whether to front-foot or absorb the cycle.
[03014] Housing Secretary: Since the Renters Reform Bill failed last term, section-21 no-fault eviction notices are up 38%. Two homelessness charities have written formally asking the government to set a date for the relaunch. The Mail and the Guardian are running the same story from opposite angles. The political cost of inaction is rising weekly.
[03015] Housing Secretary: The First Time Buyers measure has lifted demand but supply hasn't followed. Average first-buyer prices have risen 6.8% in twelve months. The IFS calls the policy a transfer from non-buyers to existing owners. The opposition is briefing it as proof the government cannot do housing.
[03016] Housing Secretary: The Renters Reform Bill did not pass last term, but the rental sector has adapted around the existing legal framework. Voluntary good-practice codes published by the National Residential Landlords Association have improved tenancy security ratings by 14 points. A relaunch this Parliament can build from a better baseline.
[03017] Housing Secretary: The first-year evaluation of the Renters Rights Bill is on my desk. Eviction notices are down 22% against the pre-Act baseline; rental advertising volumes are stable; landlord exits are within forecast. We have a defensible record to brief from.
[03018] Housing-association tenants had been deliberately excluded from the right that council tenants enjoyed since 1980. We ended the distinction. The first enfranchisements have completed and the discount payments are flowing. The record is in the deeds office filings.
[03019] I am committing today to a six-point emergency plan for the NHS: extra winter capacity, 10,000 additional beds, an immediate recruitment drive for nurses and doctors, pay settlement acceleration, elective waiting list reduction targets, and a published fortnightly performance dashboard. Every element is costed and published on the government website by this evening.
[03020] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. 20,000 additional police officers are in service. Neighbourhood policing is rebuilt. Counter-terror capability is funded, specialist knife-crime teams are operational, and the visible police presence the country has been asking for is on the streets. The Force complement is, for the first time in fifteen years, above the level the population growth would have required.
[03021] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. Corporation tax is at 21%. British businesses are now competitive with their European peers. The inward investment numbers in the months since the rate change have come in materially above the Treasury's central forecast. The decision was the right one, and the data is now confirming it.
[03022] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. Council Finance Settlement Reform is in force. The rolling 4-year settlement gives local authorities the planning horizon they always lacked. The ring-fenced social-care funding stream survives the political cycle. The statutory mechanism to address Section 114 risk catches the insolvency cases before they materialise. Local government finance is on a sustainable footing for the first time in a decade.
[03023] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. Electoral reform is law. Single transferable vote will be the basis of the next general election. The wasted-vote problem that has defined British politics for a century is on the way to resolution. This Parliament will be remembered for the structural reform it had the courage to legislate; very few are.
[03024] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. Foreign Aid Restoration is law. The 0.7% of GNI commitment is back in force. The cut imposed by the previous government is reversed. British international leadership has the instruments it always required. The tabloid headlines have been predictable; the diplomatic conversation has shifted significantly.
[03025] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. Full-expensing is permanent. Accelerated R&D credits are operational. The structural marginal rate on productive investment is materially lower, and the Treasury's own modelling shows the productivity offset banking the revenue cost over five years. British productivity finally has a tax framework that backs it.
[03026] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. Great British Energy is operational. A publicly-owned generation company, building clean power assets, returning profits to the bill-payers who actually use the electricity rather than to shareholders in offshore investment vehicles. The first projects are under construction and the first revenue is returning to consumers. The British argument about energy ownership is resolved in favour of the bill-payer.
[03027] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. Minimum Service Levels are in force. Health, transport, and education maintain a statutory floor of service during industrial action. Commuters and businesses have predictability. The strike-as-pressure-tactic on uninvolved third parties is rebalanced toward dialogue between employers and unions.
[03028] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. Net zero 2040 is in primary legislation. The sectoral carbon budgets are set, the phase-out of new petrol and diesel vehicles is law, and the trajectory that climate scientists have asked us to choose is now the trajectory the country is on. This was not a popular bill in every quarter. It was the right one.
[03029] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. Photo voter ID is in operation. The free voter authority certificate is accessible at every council office. Turnout was within the historic range and the disenfranchisement scenarios warned of by the critics did not materialise. Electoral integrity reform is now an operational fact.
[03030] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. Planning acceleration is law. Statutory decision deadlines bind every local authority. The grounds for objection are restricted to substantive material considerations. Housing starts above the local plan are unblocked. The Treasury's own inflation forecast reflects the supply-side benefit. This was a bill the country needed.
[03031] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. Private rent controls are in force. Annual rent increases capped to inflation. National landlord register operational. The transparency the rental market has resisted for thirty years is now legally required. The renting generation, after a long wait, has actual statutory protection.
[03032] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The 2.5% of GDP defence floor is in primary legislation. The new frigate programme is funded, Army recruitment is on the trajectory we set, and the NATO obligations are being met in full. British defence policy is now coherent with the strategic environment rather than wishful in the face of it. Our allies have noticed; our adversaries have also noticed.
[03033] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The AI Safety Institute is on statutory footing. Binding pre-deployment evaluation. Mandatory audit access above the 10^25 FLOP threshold. British AI safety policy no longer depends on the voluntary cooperation of companies whose interests run the other way. The Institute can act, and is acting.
[03034] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Apprenticeship Expansion is in force. The levy is doubled, 500,000 new starts per year is the delivery rate, and T-levels are operational in every FE college. The skills gap that held back productivity for a generation is being closed at structural scale. The starts are with employers in the regions and sectors with the deepest shortages.
[03035] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Auto-Deport Foreign Offenders Act is in force. Any foreign national sentenced to imprisonment now faces automatic deportation, with the human rights appeal route narrowed in primary legislation. The courts are applying the framework, the deportation figures are responding visibly, and the political commitment is now matched by operational reality.
[03036] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Benefits Uprating is delivered. Pensions and working-age benefits have risen in line with the higher of inflation or earnings. The protection promised to people on fixed incomes is the protection they have received. The triple lock for pensions, and the wider uprating principle, are honoured for another year.
[03037] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Brownfield Planning Reform Act is in force. Brownfield sites default to permitted development. Contamination remediation funding is allocated. The principle that we densify where we already have infrastructure rather than expand where we do not is now embedded in the planning system.
[03038] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Business Investment Incentives Act is law. Full-expensing renewed. Capital allowances aligned with investment cycles. The predictability the corporate sector spent five years asking for is delivered. The CBI investment intentions index has responded measurably.
[03039] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Business Rates Reform is law. The 1990s system is replaced. A land-value-based levy on commercial property is the new basis. Administration and 100% of the revenue are devolved to combined authorities. The structural unfairness between high-street and online retail is materially closed.
[03040] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Capital Gains Tax cut is in force. The transaction volumes have risen, the investment-base has broadened, and the inward investment figures are responding. Supply-side tax design works when the policy respects how investors actually behave rather than pretending they will absorb whatever rate is imposed.
[03041] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Channel Maritime Enforcement Act is in force. The Royal Navy has the legal authority to intercept and turn back small boats in the Channel. The political commitment to control the maritime border is now matched by operational reality. The crossings figures are responding visibly to the new posture.
[03042] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Council Tax revaluation is in force. Property bands now reflect actual property values rather than the values of 1991. New top bands H through Z have been added; the bottom bands have been trimmed. The revenue is now distributed in line with actual property wealth, phased over five years. The country is still here, and councils have a fiscal base that means something for the first time in a generation.
[03043] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Deepfake Criminalisation Act is law. Creation and distribution of non-consensual deepfake imagery is a criminal offence. The 24-hour statutory takedown obligation on platforms is operational. Mandatory minimum sentences apply for production of intimate-image deepfakes. The technology that enabled a new category of abuse now meets a legal architecture designed to stop it.
[03044] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Disability Rights Reform Act is law. Accessibility compliance enforcement is strengthened. The statutory deadline for transport network accessibility is set. The right to challenge benefit decisions with paid representation is operational. Disability charities had been asking for this for fifteen years; the country has acted.
[03045] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Domestic Abuse Strengthening Protections Act is in force. The new offences for coercive control are in operation, 500 IDVA posts are funded and filled, and the statutory employer-leave provisions are being followed. The cross-party support this bill received was matched by a cross-party commitment to deliver it, and we have.
[03046] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The English Devolution Act is in force. Twelve new combined authorities operational, twelve elected mayors in place, and the transport, housing, and skills budgets that used to be controlled from Whitehall are now controlled by people my constituents elected and can throw out. Whitehall did not enjoy the transition. The country will.
[03047] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Facial Recognition Ban is in force. Live facial-recognition surveillance in public spaces is prohibited. The statutory right to algorithmic explanation applies to consequential automated decisions. The civil-liberty implications of the technology are addressed in primary legislation rather than left to operational discretion.
[03048] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The First-Time Buyers Act is in force. The shared-ownership expansion is delivered, the deposit-support scheme is operational, and the government-backed mortgage guarantees for 500,000 buyers are in place. The housing-ladder bottom rung is finally within reach for the generation that has been waiting longest. The early uptake figures are at the upper end of the projection.
[03049] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Food Standards Act is in force. Chlorinated chicken imports are banned in primary legislation. Country-of-origin labelling is mandatory on every product. The Food Standards Agency has strengthened powers and the budget to use them. British food standards are now a sovereign decision rather than a chip in a trade negotiation.
[03050] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Gambling Act Reform is in force. Stake limits on online slots, mandatory affordability checks, and the operator levy on the gambling industry funding addiction treatment. The industry is in the regulatory architecture that its harms warranted for two decades. The industry warned of job losses; the early figures suggest the warning was inflated.
[03051] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Knife Crime Prevention Act is in force. Zombie knives and machetes are banned. Knife crime prevention orders are operational. The youth intervention programmes are funded. The whole-system response that the policing community and the families of victims have been asking for is now an integrated programme. The early data is moving in the right direction.
[03052] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Medicines Fair Pricing Act is law. The NHS no longer pays whatever the patent holder demands; it pays an evidence-based fair value reflecting the actual cost of development and a reasonable return. The compulsory licensing provision for life-saving drugs is operational. The scandal of patients being priced out of treatments their NHS could not afford is over.
[03053] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Mental Health Act reform is in force. Detention powers are modernised, the use of community treatment orders for under-18s is banned outright, and the statutory right to independent advocacy is established for every detained patient. The 1983 Act badly needed this update, and forty years was long enough to wait.
[03054] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The NHS Annual Uplift is in force. The above-inflation increase is reaching trusts, the waiting-list reductions are tracking the projection, and the priority the country has consistently voted for is being delivered by the government the country actually elected. The NHS is funded as a settled priority of British public spending, not a political football.
[03055] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The NHS Insurance Reform Act is law. The funding model is sustainable for the demographic challenge that every projection in the last decade made clear was coming. The co-payment thresholds protect the lowest-income households. The political taboo on honest NHS funding conversations is broken, and the conversation is happening in primary legislation rather than behind closed Treasury doors.
[03056] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Police Reform Act is in force. Body-worn cameras are mandatory. Independent misconduct panels are operational. Automatic suspension for serious allegations is in primary legislation. The public confidence that policing depends on has the structural foundations to be rebuilt. The Police Federation opposed every measure; the police chiefs supported them.
[03057] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Public Sector Pay Award is delivered. Above-inflation increases. Industrial unrest of the previous cycle is over. The Pay Review Bodies have been listened to rather than ignored. Public services are staffed by people fairly paid for the work they do; the productivity response confirms it.
[03058] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Recall of MPs Expansion is law. The threshold is lowered to 5% of constituents. The triggering offences are expanded to include serious misconduct, not just convictions. The Recall is no longer ornamental; it is a working accountability mechanism.
[03059] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Right to Disconnect Act is in force. Workers have a legal right to disconnect from work communications outside contracted hours. The enforcement mechanism through the tribunal system is operational. The business lobby called the policy unworkable; the workforce productivity figures suggest, instead, it has worked.
[03060] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Sentencing Modernisation Act is in force. Community sentences are expanded. The short-prison-term threshold is raised to 12 months. Problem-solving courts are operational. The sentencing framework reflects the evidence on what actually reduces re-offending rather than what feels good in a press release.
[03061] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Single-Use Plastics Prohibition is in force. Five-year phase-out. Producers fund the transition through extended producer responsibility. The plastic externality that British consumers and the marine environment absorbed for decades is internalised. The packaging redesign has progressed faster than the food sector predicted.
[03062] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Skills Visa Expansion is in force. The Skilled Worker route is broadened. Shortage occupations have a lower salary threshold. STEM and healthcare workers are fast-tracked. The labour-market gap which has been pushing wage inflation in critical sectors is being addressed honestly. Migration policy and economic policy are aligned.
[03063] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Supply Chain Diversification Bill is in force. Contingency supply chains for semiconductors, rare earths, and pharmaceuticals are operational. Government procurement is being diversified away from single-source suppliers. Friend-shoring grants are flowing. British industrial sovereignty is built into the procurement architecture rather than left to chance.
[03064] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Veterans Welfare Act is law. Statutory duty of care to veterans is in force. Guaranteed housing priority is operational. The mental health support pathway is funded. The employment guarantees are delivering. The cross-party support this bill received reflected the cross-society commitment that the country has long professed and not always delivered.
[03065] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The Water Industry Sewage and Standards Act is in force. The statutory ban on sewage dumping is real, the automatic per-discharge fines are paid, and the infrastructure investment is ring-fenced in law. Water companies that breach the regulations now pay before they pay shareholders. That is a change of considerable consequence.
[03066] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The annual Police Recruitment Round is delivered. Officers and PCSOs trained and in service. The force expansion is now on a sustained delivery cadence rather than a single-cycle headline. Communities, in particular the rural and small-town ones the previous decade depleted, are seeing the visible police presence they have been asking for.
[03067] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The pension reform is law. The state pension age is on a sustainable trajectory, automatic enrolment from 18 is in force, and the demographic arithmetic that every previous Chancellor preferred to pretend did not exist is honestly addressed. The actuaries are relieved, and that should be enough indication that the policy is right.
[03068] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. The two-child limit is abolished. The most cost-effective single intervention against child poverty available to any government is now in force, and 300,000 children are out of poverty as a direct and measurable consequence. That is what evidence-based welfare policy delivers when government decides to act on the evidence.
[03069] I am grateful to my honourable Friend. Votes at 16 is law. 16- and 17-year-olds will vote in all UK elections from the next cycle. Automatic voter registration captures every newly-eligible citizen at the point of leaving school. The political maturity this country claims to recognise in its young people is now matched by an actual vote.
[03070] I am not asking for a blank cheque. I am asking for a mandate to do three specific things: bring the NHS waiting list down, get 300,000 homes built a year, and balance the books by the end of the parliament. Three promises, on the record tonight, judge us on them. That is the deal.
[03071] I am not going to dress this up. I know what it is like to open a gas bill and feel it in your chest. I know what it is like to watch your wages stretch thinner every month. That is why I do this job. Not for the House of Lords, not for after-dinner speeches. For the people at home tonight wondering whether any of this actually makes a difference. It can. It will. Vote for us on Thursday.
[03072] I am not going to pretend the last few years have been easy. A pandemic, an energy shock, a war in Europe. I will tell you honestly: it has been hard. But we kept the lights on, we kept people in work, and we are coming out the other side. That matters more than any spin.
[03073] I am not going to stand here and tell you we have got everything right. We have not. I have learned a great deal in this job, and some of it the hard way. What I can promise you is this: if you give us another term, it will be a humbler, sharper government than the one you have had. I will listen more, say less, and focus on what actually matters to people. Thank you.
[03074] I appointed an independent adviser to establish the facts and I am going to let them do that. Rushing to judgement on the basis of further leaked information would not serve justice or the public interest. The review will report at the end of the week and I will act on its findings.
[03075] I have asked {minister} to step back from ministerial duties while an independent review establishes the full facts. I want to be clear: if the allegations are confirmed, the consequences will be serious. But I will not conduct a trial by newspaper. The review will report within seven days.
[03076] I have spent thirty years writing papers about what would happen if Britain switched to proportional representation. Tonight, those papers are obsolete. Every one of them. We are watching political science, live, and the results are not what any of us predicted. I want to say that on the record, before the post-hoc rationalisations begin.
[03077] I have spoken to the Prime Minister and issued a full apology on behalf of the government. The language attributed to {minister} was wrong and does not reflect Britain's view of a vital ally. We will be working in the coming days to restore trust at every level of the relationship.
[03078] I have spoken to {minister} and I am satisfied that there is important context the reporting has chosen to leave out. They have my full confidence. We are in the middle of an election campaign and I will not allow the opposition's media allies to derail it with a partial story.
[03079] I have spoken to {minister} and accepted their resignation with immediate effect. I have asked the Independent Adviser on Ministers' Interests to review the full record. The standards this government holds itself to are non-negotiable, and I will not allow this campaign to be defined by one person's misjudgement.
[03080] I said this morning that I had confidence in {minister} and that position has not changed. This is a politically motivated campaign by a newspaper with a clear agenda. I will not sack a minister on the basis of anonymous briefings and partial evidence. The British public will see this for what it is.
[03081] I want to give you a proper answer, not a soundbite. When we made that pledge, the deficit was forecast to fall. It came in far higher. Delivering it on top of the real numbers would have meant cuts to the NHS that I was not prepared to make. That was the choice. I would make it again.
[03082] I want to speak directly to anyone under thirty watching tonight. You have been told this country is in decline. I do not accept that. Your generation is the best-educated, most tolerant, most ambitious this country has ever produced. My job is to build a Britain that is worthy of you. That is what the next five years are about. Do not let anyone tell you to settle for less.
[03083] I want to talk about the young people who do not go to university, because for too long they have been an afterthought. A proper technical and apprenticeship route, valued as highly as a degree, so every young person has a path. That is the country I want to build.
[03084] I will give you the honest answer politicians dodge. You cannot have Scandinavian public services on American tax rates. We funded the health service and protected pensions, and that costs money. If the opposition want to cut tax, let them name the hospital or the pension they would cut to pay for it.
[03085] I will not pretend a uniform on every corner is enough. People want to know that when they report a crime, something happens. So: a guaranteed response to every burglary, real consequences for antisocial behaviour, and a justice system that puts the victim first. That is the standard I will hold us to.
[03086] INCOMING STORIES: If the whip has intelligence on a story about to break in the press, it appears here. Tap "Spike" to kill it before it escalates. This is your one shot to prevent a story becoming a full scandal. If you miss it, the scandal fires on the Scandals screen instead.
[03087] In light of the further information that has come to light, I no longer believe it is tenable for {minister} to remain in position even in a suspended capacity. I have accepted their resignation. The Independent Adviser will now have full access to all relevant records.
[03088] India CEPA upgraded, Australia FTA deepened, US sectoral deals on the table. The DBT thinks you can land three within the quarter. Cabinet wants a flagship press event; the FCDO wants quiet sequencing; the Treasury wants Treasury talking points to dominate.
[03089] Inherited projects: in some game modes you start with a large project already under way (e.g., HS2). You can continue it or cancel it. Cancelling stops the spending straight away and charges a one-off cancellation cost. You give up the growth the finished project would bring, it costs political capital, and the opposition and the press will hold you to it.
[03090] Islamabad will not sign a settlement that convicts it in its first clause; attribution written before any inquiry is a verdict. A chair that has itself proposed a neutral inquiry is heard differently on this line, and the inquiry version keeps Pakistan in the room.
[03091] It looks like you might be using shared value's .value inside reanimated inline style. If you want a component to update when shared value changes you should use the shared value directly instead of its current state represented by `.value`. See documentation here: https://docs.swmansion.com/react-native-reanimated/docs/fundamentals/glossary/#animations-in-inline-styling
[03092] Justice Secretary: First-year framework operations: 11 capital convictions; safeguards triggered in 3; no executions of cases later overturned. The Royal Commission on Capital Sentencing has issued an interim report that is measured-positive on operations. Public support: 58%.
[03093] Kate Holloway: An open letter signed by 18 leave-era politicians and commentators has been published in the Sunday papers, calling the accession "a mistake the country will spend a decade unwinding". Reform UK is using it in every campaign. The Telegraph leader endorses the letter. Number 10 needs an aggressive response or this becomes the long-tail political ammunition.
[03094] Kate Holloway: First petitions under the expanded Recall framework have been filed. Three reached the 5% trigger threshold; two crossed it; both MPs (one ours, one opposition) accepted by-elections. Public approval of the framework is +28. The constitutional reform is bedding in cleanly.
[03095] Kate Holloway: The Dignity in Dying campaign has formally paused its parliamentary push for the rest of this Parliament. They acknowledge the political case wasn't made last term and intend to spend two years on public opinion before re-raising. A breathing space.
[03096] Kate Holloway: The scandal you handled quietly last term has fallen out of the news cycle entirely. Lobby briefings no longer mention it. The journalist who was sniffing around it has moved to a different beat. It is, as far as Westminster is concerned, dead.
[03097] Kate Holloway: Two years after restored Single Market access, the ONS quarterly figures are unambiguous. UK exports to the EU up 31%; FDI up 22%; the long-run growth model upgraded by 0.4 percentage points. The IFS, OBR and CBI have all issued statements treating the accession as a settled positive. The Telegraph still publishes opinion pieces against; the news pages have stopped.
[03098] Kate Holloway: Two years after restored Single Market access, the political settlement around it has not held. Reform UK leads national polling on 32%. The Conservative party has formally adopted a leave-again position. Number 10 is facing the prospect of fighting the next election on the same fundamental question the country had supposedly answered. The Sunday Times has the "rejoin was a mistake" framing on permanent loop.
[03099] Let me be precise, because the number is thrown around carelessly. We cut National Insurance twice, we have taken the lowest earners out of tax altogether, and the burden is high for one honest reason: we chose to fund the NHS and pensions through a pandemic rather than slash them. I will defend that choice anywhere.
[03100] Let me be unambiguous. I am committing tonight to a clear path to two and a half per cent of GDP on defence, with the procurement reform to make every pound count. Our support for our allies has not wavered and will not. In a dangerous world, that is exactly the seriousness the moment demands.
